}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates the OSKAR architecture, a multimodal model processing video, skeleton, and text inputs through projection modules, a fusion encoder, and target encoders, with prediction and loss components. The layout is divided into three main vertical sections: input tokens on the left, a top fusion branch with an EMA update mechanism in the center, and a right prediction and loss branch. Inputs include video tokens (red), skeleton tokens (yellow), and text tokens (blue), shown as stacked rectangles with corresponding color-coded legend. These tokens feed into three parallel projection blocks — Vision Projection (red), Motion Projection (yellow), Language Projection (blue) — each transforming their respective token type into a unified representation. These projections converge into a large 'Fusion Encoder' block (gray rectangle labeled f_θ), which generates a fused representation H. This fusion output feeds into three predictors — Vision Predictor, Motion Predictor, Lang. Predictor — each producing a predicted output Y^p. These predictions are compared against ground truth targets Y^t via MSE Loss modules, one per modality (red, yellow, blue), forming the supervision loop. Below the fusion encoder, a separate branch contains 'Target Encoders' — Vision Target Encoder, Motion Target Encoder, Language Target Encoder — which are updated via Exponential Moving Average (EMA) from the fusion encoder's parameters. These target encoders are used only during fine-tuning. The entire structure is annotated with labels such as g_θ, E^f, f_θ, Q_m, and EMA to denote parameter sets and operations. The diagram uses arrows to indicate data flow: red arrows from inputs to vision projection, yellow to motion, blue to language; dashed arrows show EMA updates; solid arrows connect fusion outputs to predictors and predictors to loss functions. The legend at bottom-left clarifies token types: red = Video Token, yellow = Skeleton Token, blue = Text Token, light gray = Fused Token, white = Dropped Token, dark gray = Mask Token."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents the OWMM Agent Framework, divided into two main vertical sections: 'World Space' on the left and 'Agent Space' on the right. The World Space visually represents the environment through a top-down floor plan of a multi-room house with labeled points A, B, C, D, and a 3D rendering of an egocentric frame at point C. Above this floor plan is a 'Graph of Posed Frames' showing a sequence of static, pre-mapped images connected by dotted lines indicating temporal progression (t+1, t+2), and a 'Real-time Ego-centric Frame' shown as a live camera view from the robot's perspective. These visual elements are linked via dashed lines to the corresponding locations on the floor plan, illustrating how the robot’s viewpoint corresponds to spatial positions. The Agent Space on the right begins with an 'Instruction' box stating 'Move <Novel Object> from the <Novel Receptacle> to the <Unseen Place>', which feeds into an 'OWMM-VLM' module depicted as a neural network icon. This module receives input from both the instruction and a 'History' component (represented as a memory chip icon) and outputs into a 'Chain-of-Thought Reasoning with Multimodal Action' section. This section is subdivided into three horizontal modules: 'Ego-centric frame', 'CoT Reasoning', and 'Multimodal Action Generation'. Each row corresponds to a scenario (A, B, C, D) based on the robot's perception. For example, scenario A shows 'No relevant object' leading to 'search map loc', while scenario B indicates 'Too far to pick' leading to 'nav to point', and scenario C shows 'Can pickobject' leading to 'pick', and scenario D shows 'Can place object' leading to 'place'. Each action is paired with a corresponding visual frame from the ego-centric view and an icon representing the action (magnifying glass, location pin, robotic arm). All these reasoning outputs feed into a final 'Learning-free positional-based robot planners' block at the bottom, which executes the actions. Arrows indicate the flow of information: from the world space to the agent space, from instruction/history to OWMM-VLM, from OWMM-VLM to CoT reasoning, and from CoT reasoning to multimodal actions and finally to the robot planners. The entire framework illustrates a closed-loop system where perception, reasoning, and action planning are integrated for robotic manipulation tasks."
}

