{
  "fig_desc": "The figure is divided into two main sections by a vertical dashed line, labeled 'Learn temporal distance representations' on the left and 'Extract goal-reaching policy' on the right. A large black arrow points from the left section to the right, indicating a sequential workflow. In the left section, there are multiple circular nodes connected by arrows. The top node is labeled ψ(s), which has red arrows pointing down to three smaller rectangular boxes labeled φ(s,a₁), φ(s,a₂), and φ(s,a₃). These boxes have dashed arrows pointing to three black dots below them. From these black dots, dashed arrows point to another set of circular nodes labeled ψ(s'), ψ(w), and ψ(g). There is a teal-colored arrow labeled 'temporal (T) invariance' connecting ψ(s') to ψ(w), and a purple arrow labeled 'action (T) invariance' connecting ψ(s) to ψ(s'). Below this, a mathematical inequality is shown: dψ(s')→ψ(g) ≤ dψ(s')→ψ(w) + dψ(w)→ψ(g), with the caption 'triangle inequality with quasimetric architecture (P-invariance)' underneath. In the bottom-left corner, a box contains '+ time-contrastive MC loss' and 'ψ(s_{t+k}) predicts φ(s_t, a)', indicating the training objective. On the right side, under 'Extract goal-reaching policy', there are similar circular nodes: φ(s,a₁), φ(s,a₂), φ(s,a₃), ψ(g), and an additional teal circle labeled φ(s,a₂). Dotted lines connect φ(s,a₁) to φ(s,a₂) and φ(s,a₂) to ψ(g), forming a curved path. A solid green arrow points from φ(s,a₁) to φ(s,a₂), and another solid green arrow points from φ(s,a₂) to ψ(g). Below this, the optimization objective is written as 'min_a dφ(s,a)→ψ(g)', followed by '⇒ π(s) ← a₂', indicating the extracted policy selects action a₂. The entire figure illustrates how a temporal distance representation is learned and then used to derive a goal-conditioned policy."
