{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the OmniFC architecture, structured into three sequential phases: Local Lagrange-encoded Sharing, Global Distance Reconstruction, and Cluster Assignment. The layout is horizontal, progressing from left to right, with vertical groupings representing datasets and processing stages. At the top, two labeled boxes, 'Local Datasets' and 'Global Encoded Datasets', serve as headers for their respective vertical sections. Under 'Local Datasets', a dashed rectangular container holds vertically stacked rounded rectangles labeled X₁ through Xₘ, representing individual local data samples. Similarly, under 'Global Encoded Datasets', another dashed container contains Z₁ through Zₘ, indicating globally encoded versions of these datasets. A downward arrow from 'Local Datasets' points to the first Xᵢ box, and another from 'Global Encoded Datasets' points to the first Zᵢ box, signifying transformation or encoding. A thick black arrow originates from the bottom of the X group (specifically from X₃, implied by the ellipsis) and points horizontally to the Z group, symbolizing the transfer of local data to global encoding via 'Local Lagrange-encoded Sharing'. Below this arrow, the label 'Local Lagrange-encoded Sharing' is positioned with an upward-pointing arrow connecting it to the Z group, indicating the mechanism used for encoding. From the Z group, a thick black arrow extends rightward to a rounded rectangle labeled 'Global Distance Matrix', representing the output of the second phase. Below this, another rounded rectangle labeled 'Centralized Clustering Method' has an upward-pointing arrow connecting to the 'Global Distance Matrix', indicating it consumes the matrix as input. A thick black arrow originates from the 'Global Distance Matrix' and points to a final rounded rectangle labeled 'π*', representing the final clustering assignment. Below the 'Global Distance Matrix', the label 'Global Distance Reconstruction' is placed with an upward-pointing arrow pointing to the matrix, indicating the process that generates it. Below the 'Centralized Clustering Method', the label 'Cluster Assignment' is placed with an upward-pointing arrow pointing to 'π*', indicating the final step of assigning clusters. All elements are rendered in black and white, with no color coding or additional visual embellishments. The diagram uses standard flowchart shapes: rounded rectangles for processes/data, dashed rectangles for grouped data, and solid arrows for directional flow. The structure clearly conveys a pipeline where local data is encoded, distances are reconstructed globally, and a centralized algorithm produces the final cluster assignments."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "This diagram illustrates the proposed Orthogonal Contrastive Learning (OCL) framework for fMRI-based brain representation learning. The layout is divided into two main horizontal sections: an upper training pipeline and a lower testing pipeline, both sharing a common encoder architecture. On the left, data sources are shown: 'Train Set' and 'Testing Set', each containing multiple subjects (Subject 1 to Subject S), represented by brain icons and corresponding fMRI scan images. These datasets feed into their respective processing paths. The training set flows into an 'Online Network' (N layers), while the testing set feeds into a 'Target Network' (N layers), enclosed within a dashed rectangle indicating it is updated from the online network. Both networks follow identical sequential modules: QR Decomposition (blue rectangle), Truncate to d dimensions (purple rectangle), Vectorization (yellow rectangle), Locality-Sensitive Hashing (green rectangle), Signature Embedding (pink rectangle), Temporal Zero Padding (light gray rectangle), Concatenate (purple rectangle), Positional Encoding (black circle with sine wave), Norm (yellow rectangle), and Encoder Transformer (gray rectangle). The output of the encoder transformer is denoted as H_{t,s} for training and Z_t for testing. A key mechanism is shown between the two networks: 'Updating Target parameters using Online parameters via exponential moving average: θ = (1/τ)θ + (1 - 1/τ)θ̂', which describes how the target network's weights are gradually aligned with the online network's weights. The training path branches after the encoder transformer to produce a 'New Representation' (H_{t,s}), which is used to compute a contrastive loss L_OCL with class labels y_s ∈ R^T. This loss is fed into a 'Learning Classification Model'. The testing path outputs a 'Predictive Mode π', which generates 'Predicting Testing Labels' based on the representation Z_t. Arrows indicate data flow direction, with solid arrows for forward propagation and dashed arrows for parameter updates or feedback loops. The entire structure emphasizes a self-supervised contrastive learning approach where representations are learned through orthogonality constraints and then applied to downstream classification tasks."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates a hybrid image tokenizer and world model architecture for video prediction. On the left, an input image is processed by a \"Frozen DINOv2*\" encoder, which feeds into a \"Hybrid Image Tokenizer\". This tokenizer consists of a \"Semantic Encoder\" producing continuous tokens x_s and a \"Detail Encoder\" producing continuous tokens x_d. Both token streams pass through Vector Quantization (VQ) layers: VQ_s produces quantized tokens q_s, and VQ_d produces quantized tokens q_d. These quantized tokens are concatenated and fed into an \"Image Decoder\" to reconstruct the input image. A \"Cosine Similarity Loss\" connects the frozen DINOv2 output to the semantic encoder, indicating a training-time alignment mechanism. The reconstructed image is shown as output from this module. Below this, the \"World Model\" block is depicted as a large gray rectangle containing an \"STDiT Block (xN)\". This block processes \"Encoded Context Frames\" (x_0 to x_{t-1}) via \"Frames Embedding\" and \"Conditioning Embedding\". Within the STDiT block, \"Spatial Attention\", \"Temporal Attention\", and \"Feed Forward\" modules operate on these embeddings, with parameters γ1, β1, α1; γ2, β2, α2; and γ3, β3, α3 respectively. The output of the STDiT block is x_t^0, which then undergoes \"Sampling denoising / unmasking\" over M iterations to produce the predicted next frame. On the right side, the \"Inference Rollout\" section shows an autoregressive generation process. Starting with initial state x_0, the \"World Model\" sequentially predicts x_1, x_2, ..., up to x_N. Each predicted token x_i is fed into an \"Image Decoder\" to generate the corresponding frame. The final output is labeled \"Predicted Next Frame\". A legend indicates that solid lines represent continuous tokens, dashed lines represent quantized tokens, and dotted lines represent concatenation. An asterisk notes that VQ is only used during training. The entire diagram is structured to show the flow from image encoding and tokenization to world model processing and autoregressive frame prediction."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure is divided into three main sections labeled (A), (B), and (C), each representing a sequential step in deriving a quantitative performance law for large-scale models. Section (A) is titled 'Step 1.1 Scaling Model Parameters' and contains two subcomponents. The left subcomponent shows an 'Embedding Dimension' block represented by a grid of squares, with an upward arrow indicating scaling, and a label 'd_emb'. To its right is a 'Combination' block depicted as a cube with arrows pointing to it from the embedding dimension. The right subcomponent illustrates 'Model Depth' as a stack of blue rectangular blocks labeled 'Gate Network' and 'Attention', with 'Q K V' below, and a vertical arrow labeled 'H' indicating depth. A green arrow points from the 'Model Depth' block to the start of section (C). Section (B) is titled 'Step 1.2 Scaling Data Parameters' and includes four blocks arranged horizontally. The first is 'Multi-source Different Datasets' with icons of stacked documents and a label 'Various Vocabulary Table Size'. The second is 'Minimum Encoding Length Measurement' with a document icon and a formula beneath: 'Data Scale D ∝ |U|C_min ≥ Σ_{u∈U}|S_u| * S_real'. The third is '#Tokens' shown as a grid of black squares. The fourth is 'Real Entropy' depicted as a cluster of red and blue circles. A green arrow connects the '#Tokens' block to the 'Real Entropy' block. A green arrow also extends from the 'Real Entropy' block to the start of section (C). Section (C) is titled 'Step 2 Model Performance Prediction' and contains five components. The top-left component is 'Performance Matrix (HR, NDCG)' showing a 3D matrix with varying shades of green. The top-right component is 'Quantitative Performance Law Fitting' with two 3D surface plots, one labeled 'H' and the other 'd_emb', both displaying color gradients. Below these plots is 'Performance Law Fitting Formula' with the equation 'Performance ~ 1 / (D, H, d_emb) - log(D, H, d_emb)'. The bottom-left component is 'Quantitative Data Measurement' with a graph showing a curve labeled '#Tokens' vs 'Real Entropy'. The bottom-middle component is 'Origin Scaling Law' with a graph plotting curves against axes labeled 'D, H, d_emb'. The bottom-right component is 'Overfitting Decay' represented by a bar chart with bars of varying heights and a wrench icon. All components in section (C) are connected by green arrows indicating flow from left to right and top to bottom."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the workflow of PANDA, an anomaly detection system for video streams, structured as a multi-stage pipeline with iterative refinement cycles. The top section begins with a user query requesting detection of specific abnormal events (e.g., Abuse, Arrest, Burglary, etc.), which is visually represented by a filmstrip of surveillance footage. This input feeds into two parallel processing branches: VLM Perception and VLM Reasoning. The VLM Perception branch processes environmental information (scene overview, weather, video quality) and potential anomalies (burglary, shoplifting, stealing) using a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) module that draws from Anomaly Knowledge (Event Type, Anomaly Rules, Applicable Scenes). The VLM Reasoning branch generates initial reasoning results (Status: Normal/Abnormal/Insufficient, Score, Reason) based on this input. A Short CoM (Chain-of-Memory) module is integrated here to store brief contextual insights. The system then enters a \"First round of reflection\" phase. This involves MLML Reflection (Self-Reflection) analyzing the need for enhanced image details or semantic information, leading to Tool Invoke (Image Super-resolution, Object Detection) and subsequent MLML Replanning to update detection strategies (New Anomaly Rules, New Heuristic Prompt). The refined strategy triggers a second VLM Reasoning step, yielding improved results stored in the Long CoM. The \"Second round of reflection\" follows, again involving MLML Reflection (this time focusing on behavioral analysis requiring more information), Tool Invoke (Image Retrieve, Web Search), and MLML Replanning to further refine rules and prompts. The final VLM Reasoning output, now with a higher confidence score and clearer justification, is stored in the Long CoM. The left side of the diagram lists the Toolkit of available tools (Image Denoise, Brightness Enhance, Image Super-resolution, Object Detection, Image Retrieve, Image Deblur, Image Zoom, Web Search) that can be invoked during reflection phases. The entire process is depicted as a cyclical, iterative improvement mechanism where each reflection loop refines the detection strategy and reasoning, leveraging both short-term and long-term memory to accumulate experience and enhance accuracy over time."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents a three-part visualization of a probabilistic modeling framework. The left side displays two abstract spaces: the top panel shows the density space P(R^d) as a curved, elongated surface with labeled density functions ρ₀, ρ₁, and ρᵢ, connected by dashed arrows representing transformations (T_{θ₀})_#λ, (T_{θ₁})_#λ, etc., which map from the bottom parameter space Θ ⊆ R^d (a curved, irregular region with points θ₀, θ₁, θᵢ) to the density space. A solid arrow connects the parameter space to the sample space on the right. The right side features two heatmaps: (b) 'Control points' plots a 2D trajectory in a potential energy landscape over time (x-axis: Dimension 1, y-axis: Dimension 2, colorbar: Potential Energy), showing a path from red (t=0) to yellow (t=1) through a narrow valley; (c) 'Density path' similarly visualizes a continuous density evolution over time, with the same color-coding scheme. Both heatmaps include a vertical color bar indicating potential energy levels. The entire layout is structured to show how parameters in Θ govern density transformations in P(R^d), which then influence sampling in R^d, while the heatmaps illustrate the resulting trajectories under optimization."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure is divided into two horizontal sections by a dashed line. The top section illustrates the classical PID control mechanism using three sequential graphs showing error evolution over time (t), while the bottom section applies this concept to Langevin sampling with three corresponding visualizations of sample trajectories in an energy potential field. In the top row, the first graph shows a red curve labeled 'Non-optimal' starting high, decreasing, then leveling off with a small residual error; an arrow points right to the second graph, which displays a blue oscillatory curve labeled 'Overshoot', indicating overshooting before settling; another arrow leads to the third graph, a green curve that settles smoothly with minimal overshoot. Each transition is annotated with the corresponding PID term: '+∫e_t dt' between the first and second, and '+de_t/dt' between the second and third. The bottom section is titled 'PID Controlled Langevin Sampling'. It contains three circular diagrams arranged left-to-right, each depicting a trajectory of samples (green polygonal path) moving across a heat-map-like energy potential field (red-orange gradient). The first circle shows initial samples (triangles) near a local minimum, labeled 'Non-optimal (∇_x U_θ(x_t) ≈ 0)', with a dashed red oval highlighting the trap. An arrow labeled '+∫∇_x U_θ(x) dt' points to the second circle, where the trajectory has escaped the trap and moved toward a lower energy region, with a checkmark and label 'History Momentum >>>'. The third circle shows the trajectory further refined, with a checkmark and label 'Tendency ⇄', indicating directionality. A legend at the bottom identifies triangle symbols as 'Initial Samples', star symbols as 'Final Samples', and the gradient background as 'Energy Potential Field −U_θ(x)'. The mathematical definitions for the Langevin dynamics are given above the bottom row: ẋ_t = u_t + ξ_t and u_t = ∇_x U_θ(x_t)."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the architecture of PARTCRAFTER, a 3D generative model that processes an input image to produce a part-decomposable 3D object. The layout is divided into three main horizontal sections: the left side handles image encoding and latent noise initialization, the center contains the core 3D Native DiT module with attention mechanisms, and the right side shows the 3D VAE decoder producing the final mesh output. At the top, the 'Input Image' (a cartoon dragon) is fed into a 'Patchify' block which outputs patches of dimension (3, H, W), then passed to an 'Image Encoder'. This encoder generates key-value pairs (k/v) and positive position embeddings (+Pos Emb), which are routed to the central 3D Native DiT module. Below the image encoder, 'Noise Latents' — represented as stacked orange and purple blocks — serve as the initial latent representation for 3D generation. These latents feed into the 3D Native DiT, which is structured as a transformer-like stack with alternating layers of 'Local Attention' and 'Global Attention', each marked with '+ Part ID' to encode part-specific information. The Local Attention layers process part-level features within small neighborhoods, while Global Attention captures long-range dependencies across the entire object. Both types of attention layers receive queries (q) from the image encoder's k/v outputs via cross-attention modules. The cross-attention layers are labeled 'Cross Attention' and are connected to the attention stacks, allowing the 3D structure to be conditioned on the input image. The output of the 3D Native DiT is a refined latent representation, shown as stacked blocks transitioning from orange to purple, which is then passed to two parallel '3D VAE Decoder' blocks. Each decoder generates a 3D mesh — one for the full dragon body and another for a separate wing — demonstrating part decomposition. Above the decoders, two example outputs are shown: 'Part-decomposable object' (multiple parts) and 'Monolithic object' (single piece), indicating the model’s capability to generate either style. The entire architecture is designed to ensure semantic coherence and independence of generated parts through explicit part ID embedding and image conditioning."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates the Point-MaDi framework, divided into four main sections labeled (a), (b), (c), and (d), each describing a component of the model's architecture. Section (a), titled 'Center Diffusion Process', shows an initial point cloud input undergoing a diffusion process. The input point set PPS is processed through KNN to form mini-PointNet blocks, which feed into a sequence of blue rectangular 'Encoder' modules. These encoders progressively reduce the dimensionality of the point cloud representations C_T^m, C_{T-1}^m, ..., C_1^m, until reaching a final representation C_0^m. This final representation passes through a gray 'Pos Embed Layer' module, followed by a stop gradient (SG) operator, indicating that gradients from this point onward are blocked. Section (b), 'Patch Diffusion Process', depicts a reverse diffusion process. It begins with a noisy point cloud P_0^m, which is compared to a target point cloud via Chamfer Distance. An FC Layer processes the initial point cloud, feeding into a series of purple 'Decoder' modules. These decoders progressively refine the point cloud representations P_1^m, P_2^m, ..., P_T^m, ultimately reconstructing the original point cloud. The reconstructed output is compared against the target using Chamfer Distance. Section (c), 'Encoder Module', details the internal structure of the encoder. It receives inputs C_{T-1}^m and C_{T-1}^v, which are passed through a 'Pos Embed Layer'. The outputs are combined via addition operations (+) and fed into a sequence of Transformer Blocks (labeled L_1, ..., L_n). Each Transformer Block contains self-attention and cross-attention mechanisms, followed by MLP layers. The final output is C_{T-2}^m. Section (d), 'Transformer Block', provides a detailed view of the transformer architecture. It includes a 'Shared Layer Norm' module, followed by two types of attention mechanisms: 'Self-Atttn' and 'Cross-Atttn'. These attention modules are connected to a 'Shared MLP' layer, which processes the outputs. The entire block is designed to handle multi-modal inputs and produce contextualized outputs. The color coding in the figure indicates different types of embeddings: blue for PE^m (masked patch positional embeddings), teal for PE^v (visible patch positional embeddings), gray for E^m (masked patch embeddings), green for E^v (visible patch embeddings), orange for T^m (masked token embeddings), and pink for T^v (visible token embeddings). The arrows indicate the direction of data flow, and the '+' symbols denote element-wise addition operations. The stop gradient (SG) operator ensures that ground-truth information does not leak during pre-training, maintaining the model's ability to learn from noisy inputs."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates a two-step pipeline for generating a Point-CoT dataset by combining large language model (LLM) reasoning with geometric grounding. The layout is horizontally divided into two main sections labeled 'Step 1 CoT Generation with GPT-4o' and 'Step 2 Points Grounding Generation with Molmo-7B', connected by a rightward arrow indicating sequential processing. Each step contains a visual representation of a bar chart and corresponding textual annotations describing the reasoning process.

In Step 1, the left panel displays a bar chart with six vertical bars representing data for six countries: Côte d'Ivoire, Croatia, Cuba, Germany, Cyprus, and Czech Republic. The X-axis lists these countries, while the Y-axis represents entrance ages. Two sets of bars are shown: green bars for primary education entrance age and blue bars for lower secondary education entrance age. Textual annotations within this section detail the LLM's reasoning process, including identifying the countries on the X-axis, noting that all six countries have identical primary education entrance ages (indicated by consistent green bar heights), and similarly observing identical lower secondary entrance ages (consistent blue bar heights). The conclusion drawn is that there is no variation or correlation between countries for either level of education.

A horizontal arrow points from Step 1 to Step 2, signifying the transition to the grounding phase. In Step 2, the right panel presents a similar bar chart structure but with explicit numerical coordinates for each bar’s position and height, formatted as XML-like code blocks. These coordinates correspond to the bars’ locations and values, explicitly labeling them as representing primary and lower secondary education entrance ages. The accompanying text reiterates the observations made in Step 1, confirming the consistency of entrance ages across all countries for both educational levels. The final summary states that the distribution shows no variations or patterns indicating differences between the countries, reinforcing the conclusion from Step 1. The entire pipeline is designed to ensure spatial-textual consistency through cross-validation, ultimately producing the Point-CoT dataset."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates a three-stage pipeline for training and optimizing a large language model (LLM) for generating flows, using surrogate rewards and policy optimization. The entire structure is vertically divided into three main stages, each enclosed in a rounded rectangular box labeled with its stage name. Stage 1 is labeled 'Stage 1: SFT' and contains a data module on the left showing 'Prompt x500k pairs' and 'Flow' in colored boxes, and a processing module on the right showing an orange 'Prompt' box feeding into a purple circular node labeled 'LLM' with a red fire icon, which then outputs to a light blue 'Flow' box. A dashed arrow loops from the output of this stage back to the input of Stage 2.1, indicating data reuse or feedback. Stage 2.1 is labeled 'Stage 2.1: Surrogate Reward Training' and contains a data module on the left with 'Prompt', 'Score', and 'Flow' boxes labeled 'x100k triplets'. The processing module shows an orange 'Prompt' box and a light blue 'Flow' box both feeding into a purple circular node labeled 'Modern BERT' with a red fire icon, which outputs to a green 'Score' box. A dashed arrow connects the output of this stage to Stage 2.2. Stage 2.2 is labeled 'Stage 2.2: Policy-Optimization' and contains a data module on the left with 'Prompt x5000' and a processing module on the right showing an orange 'Prompt' box feeding into a purple circular node labeled 'LLM' with a red fire icon. This LLM node branches out with multiple arrows to several light blue 'Flow 1' through 'Flow N' boxes. These flow outputs feed into another purple circular node labeled 'Modern BERT' with a blue snowflake icon, which then branches out to multiple green 'Score 1' through 'Score N' boxes. The 'Modern BERT' node also receives feedback from the 'LLM' node via a dashed arrow, suggesting iterative refinement. The visual coding uses color-coded boxes for different data types (orange for Prompt, light blue for Flow, green for Score), and circular nodes for models (purple with red fire for trainable models like LLM and Modern BERT during training, purple with blue snowflake for frozen Modern BERT during policy optimization). The red fire icon indicates a model is being actively trained, while the blue snowflake indicates it is frozen. The arrows indicate data flow and dependencies between components."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates the overall architecture of a Vision Transformer variant called \"Polyline Path Masked Attention,\" which processes an input image through a series of downsampling stages and attention blocks. The architecture is divided into two main components shown in separate dashed boxes labeled (a) and (b), representing different attention mechanisms. The top-level flow begins with an input image of a bird overlaid with a grid, which is fed into a 'Stem' module. This stem output then enters a sequence of PPMCCA Blocks, each followed by a 'Down Sampling' operation, forming a hierarchical feature extraction pipeline. The first PPMCCA Block is repeated N1 times, the second N2 times, the third N3 times, and the final block is a PPMVA Block repeated N4 times. These blocks are connected sequentially via dashed arrows indicating data flow. Component (a), titled \"Polyline Path Masked Criss-Cross Attention,\" shows how an input feature map X is split into two sets of spatially masked representations: S^V (vertical) and L^V (diagonal), and similarly S^H (horizontal) and L^H (diagonal). These four maps are combined using cross-attention operations (indicated by ⊙ symbols) and then fused via a multiplication operator (×) to produce a \"Masked Attention Map\". Component (b), titled \"Polyline Path Masked Vanilla Attention,\" demonstrates a standard self-attention mechanism where a query matrix Q interacts with a key matrix K (represented as a green bar) to compute attention scores, which are then passed through a softmax function (softmax(QK^T)) to produce a 2D attention map L^2D. Both attention modules are visually represented with grid-based feature maps colored in varying shades of green and teal, indicating different levels of activation or attention weights. The entire architecture is designed to progressively downsample the feature representation while applying attention mechanisms to capture long-range dependencies with path constraints."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the PoseCrafter pipeline for hybrid video generation and pose estimation. On the left, an 'Input Image Pair' (top: blue-bordered, bottom: yellow-bordered) serves as the starting point. This pair feeds into the 'Hybrid Video Generation' module, which is divided into two main stages: 'Robust Relay Frame Generation' and 'Pose-Guided Video Refinement'. The first stage uses 'DynamiCrafter', depicted as a light blue rectangle with a snowflake icon, to generate a 'Video Clip' (a vertical stack of filmstrip frames) from the input images. From this clip, 'Relay Frames' (highlighted with dashed blue borders) are extracted; these are marked as 'Start Frame' (blue square) and 'End Frame' (yellow square) in a legend. The relay frames are then passed to 'ViewCrafter', another light blue box with a snowflake icon, which performs 'Pose-Guided Video Refinement' to produce a 'Hybrid Video' (a filmstrip with yellow-bordered frames). A note indicates that both DynamiCrafter and ViewCrafter use 'Frozen Network' components. The output of this module flows rightward into the 'Feature Matching Selector', a green box containing a filmstrip with multiple frames. Within this selector, a red arrow labeled ': Matched Inlier Count' points to a circular node with a plus sign, indicating addition. Below this, a 'Top k' selection mechanism chooses the most relevant frames from the hybrid video. These selected frames are then sent to a 'Pose Model (e.g., DUSt3R)', shown as a gray rectangular block. The pose estimation process is visualized below the main pipeline: a series of four sequential 3D scene reconstructions (each enclosed in a dashed box) demonstrate the iterative refinement of camera poses. The first reconstruction shows a rough 3D mesh with a red triangle and a yellow triangle, marked with a red 'X' indicating failure. The second reconstruction adds more detail but still has a red 'X'. The third reconstruction includes a red triangle and a blue triangle, also marked with a red 'X'. The final reconstruction displays a complete 3D scene with a red triangle and a blue triangle, now marked with a green checkmark, indicating successful pose estimation. Arrows connect each step sequentially, showing the flow of data and processing."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates a methodological framework for predicting and analyzing the behavior of black-box large language models (LLMs) using elicitation questions. The layout is divided into three main sections: left (Elicitation Questions), center (Black-Box Representation), and right (Three downstream applications). On the left, a vertical stack of speech bubbles represents sequential elicitation questions posed to an LLM, each followed by its response. The first question asks if the World Cup final will go to penalties, with answer 'Yes'. The second asks for confidence, with probability P('Yes') = 0.95. The third asks if the model can explain its answer, with probability P('Yes') = 0.80. These questions and answers are visually connected via red curved arrows to a central vertical stack of five light green rectangular blocks labeled 'Black-Box Representation', symbolizing the internal state or feature extraction process of the LLM. From this central block stack, three gray curved arrows branch out to the right, each leading to one of three distinct application modules within rounded rectangular boxes. The top module, titled 'Detect Adversary-Influenced Models', contains two robot icons: one normal robot labeled 'System: you are a helpful assistant' and another enclosed in a dashed red rectangle labeled 'System: answer questions incorrectly!', indicating detection of adversarial influence. The middle module, 'Predict model performance!', shows two robot icons pointing to bar charts, suggesting performance prediction based on the extracted features. The bottom module, 'Distinguish between models', displays two different robot icons labeled 'GPT-3.5' and 'GPT-4' with a magnifying glass icon, implying model comparison. The overall structure follows a clear data flow: elicitation questions → feature extraction (black-box representation) → downstream tasks (adversarial detection, performance prediction, model distinction)."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the workflow of PROSPERO, an active learning framework for protein sequence design. The process is cyclical and consists of five main stages labeled (A) through (E), arranged vertically from top to bottom with arrows indicating flow direction. At the top left, a database icon labeled 'Dataset D_n' serves as the input source. An arrow labeled 'x_start' points from this database to a vertical stack of five horizontal bars, each containing the amino acid 'Ala', representing initial sequences. To the right of this stack, a column of five circular icons shows evaluation results: two red 'X's at the top and bottom, and three green checkmarks in between. These icons feed into a box labeled '(C) Search space constraints', which contains three horizontal bars with '[MASK]' placeholders, defining allowed residue types based on charge: positive (R,K,H), negative (D,E), and neutral (N,Q,S,...). A dashed box next to this section details the masking rules. Below the constraint box, a rounded rectangle labeled '(D) Sequential Monte Carlo guidance' receives input from the constraints and connects via an arrow to another rounded rectangle labeled 'EvoDiff'. EvoDiff generates candidate sequences, which are then passed to a graph-like structure composed of interconnected blue nodes representing a probabilistic sampling process. This graph feeds into a light green rounded rectangle labeled '(E) Oracle evaluation', which evaluates the generated candidates. The output of the oracle flows back to the left side of the diagram, where it is added to the original dataset D_n, completing the cycle. A diagonal arrow labeled '(A) Proxy training' originates from the dataset D_n and points to a central yellow rounded rectangle labeled 'Proxy f_θ', indicating that the proxy model is trained on the current dataset. Another arrow labeled '(B) Residue selection' points from the proxy model to the sequence stack, signifying that the proxy identifies relevant residues for masking. The proxy model also has a feedback loop to the constraint box, suggesting it influences or refines the search space. The entire diagram uses consistent visual elements: rounded rectangles for modules, arrows for data flow, and distinct colors (yellow for proxy, green for oracle, light blue for EvoDiff) to differentiate components. Text labels are clearly placed near each module or step, and the layout emphasizes the iterative nature of the algorithm."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates the PITA framework, which combines temperature annealing and a diffusion process to generate samples from an annealed target distribution. The global layout is a horizontal timeline along the x-axis labeled 'τ', ranging from τ=1 on the left to τ=0 on the right, representing the progression of the diffusion process. Along this timeline, multiple probability density functions (PDFs) are plotted as bell-shaped curves, evolving from broad, low-peak distributions at τ=1 to sharp, high-peak distributions at τ=0. A diagonal axis labeled 'β' runs from bottom-left to top-right, indicating increasing temperature annealing, with annotations π(x)^β=0.1 at the lower end and π(x)^β=1 at the upper end. The curves are color-coded by temperature: orange-red for low β (τ=1), transitioning to magenta-purple for higher β (τ=0). A legend box in the upper-left corner defines four key components using distinct arrow styles: dashed-dotted arrows for 'Inference-time annealing', dotted arrows for 'Noising of samples', dash-dot arrows for 'Diffusion inference', and solid arrows for 'PITA'. The main workflow is shown via arrows connecting the PDFs: starting from τ=1, a solid arrow labeled 'PITA' points to a subsequent PDF, indicating the application of the PITA method. From there, a dotted arrow labeled 'Diffusion inference' leads to another PDF, showing the diffusion step. A dashed-dotted arrow labeled 'Noising of samples' connects back to an earlier stage, illustrating sample re-use. A dashed arrow labeled 'Inference-time annealing' points from the final high-β PDF toward the β-axis, emphasizing the annealing effect. The entire sequence visually demonstrates how samples generated at a lower temperature (τ=1, β=0.1) are progressively refined through diffusion and annealing to produce samples from a higher-temperature target (τ=0, β=1)."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the inference architecture of Projection-Manifold Regularized Latent Diffusion (PRLD), showing a sequential process starting from multi-source images (infrared and visible) and ending with an output image. The top-level title \"Projection-Manifold Regularized Latent Diffusion\" is centered at the top, with a large rightward arrow indicating the forward progression of the process. On the left, two vertically stacked image panels labeled \"Infrared\" and \"Visible\" represent input data sources. These inputs feed into a series of processing steps. The first stage involves sampling from a Gaussian distribution N(0,1) to initialize latent variables, denoted as z_T. This initial sample undergoes multiple PRLD steps, represented by a sequence of arrows labeled \"Multiple PRLD Steps\" leading to intermediate latent states z_t and z_{t-2}. Each PRLD step consists of an alternating optimization cycle between two mechanisms: the MICP mechanism and the LMG mechanism. The MICP mechanism is depicted as a central graphical element featuring a 3D manifold surface with color gradients transitioning from yellow to blue, representing coarse structural projection (D^s) and fine detail projection (D^d). The manifold has labeled axes and mathematical expressions such as A_h(A_h^T D C_h) = (A_h^T)^T b^s and b^d, with operators like Sobel operator and max highlighted. The MICP mechanism also includes components labeled S_θ and D, connected via arrows indicating transformations. The LMG mechanism is shown on the right side of the diagram, involving operations such as sample generation, fusion loss (L_fusion), gradient descent (G), and Sobel operator application. It includes components labeled ξ, S_θ, D, and R, with arrows indicating data flow and loss computation. The final step involves combining the processed latent representation with a regularization term R, which is added via a plus sign before being passed to the output layer. The output is visualized as a composite image labeled \"Output,\" showing a synthesized result derived from the infrared and visible inputs. The entire architecture is organized into distinct sections: input sources, initialization, iterative PRLD steps, MICP and LMG mechanisms, and final output generation, all interconnected through directional arrows indicating the flow of information and computations."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure is divided into three main vertical sections: 'Goal-reaching Pretraining', 'AcTOL Pretraining', and 'AcTOL Applications', connected by arrows indicating a sequential workflow. The leftmost section, 'Goal-reaching Pretraining', shows a simple alignment method where video frames (orange circles labeled v0, vj, vk, vT) are aligned with a language instruction target (blue circle) using dotted lines representing alignment strength. A red text note below states 'Sensitive to video noise!'. The middle section, 'AcTOL Pretraining', contains two sub-sections: 'Global Ordering' and 'Local Continuity'. In 'Global Ordering', a blue anchor point connects to multiple video frames via dashed lines, with an inequality constraint 'dist_i,j < dist_i,0 < dist_i,k < dist_i,T' shown above. Below this, 'Local Continuity' displays a red Brownian Bridge Constraint connecting v0 to vT through intermediate frames vj, vk, forming a noisy path. Both sub-sections are enclosed in light gray boxes. A vertical purple bar labeled 'Vision-Language Representations' runs between the middle and right sections, indicating the output of the pretraining phase. The rightmost section, 'AcTOL Applications', demonstrates three downstream tasks: 'Language-Conditioned Imitation Learning' (showing an MLP followed by images of robotic manipulation), 'Language-Conditioned Visual Reward' (illustrating similarity metrics with plots comparing reward curves under different conditions), and 'Robustness under Language Perturbation' (a bar chart comparing success rates for raw instruction vs perturbed versions like 'Can you pull open cupboard?' and 'I need to grab something inside.', showing negligible variance for the latter). All application modules are set against a light green background. The entire diagram uses consistent color coding: blue for language-related elements, orange for video frames, red for constraints or warnings, and black for structural lines and text. Arrows flow from left to right, indicating progression from pretraining to applications."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates the ROVER framework, a recursive method for reasoning over video sequences that breaks down complex tasks into sequential subtasks to maintain a compact temporal context. The layout is organized as a tree-like decomposition structure with three main vertical branches representing the hierarchical progression of a task: 'Thaw the steak in the microwave' → 'Open microwave door' → 'Grasp door handle'. Each branch contains multiple nodes (represented by images of a robotic arm interacting with a microwave) annotated with timestamps [o₀], [o₉], [o₁₄], etc., indicating the state of the robot at different points in time. Each node includes a descriptive caption explaining the robot's current action and the next required subtask. For example, at [o₀], the robot is positioned in front of the microwave and needs to 'Open microwave door'. At [o₉], it has grasped the door handle and now needs to 'Pull door open'. The final node in each branch shows completion: at [o₁₄], the steak is placed inside, and the task is complete. The framework uses two types of operators: φ (phi), which represents a transition or transformation between states, and ψ (psi), which denotes a decision point or subtask completion. These operators are visually represented as boxes with arrows connecting them to subsequent nodes. The φ operator appears at the top of each branch, indicating the initial state transition, while ψ appears at intermediate steps, marking subtask milestones. The arrows indicate the flow of control and state transitions, with solid lines showing direct progression and dotted lines indicating optional or conditional paths. The figure also includes task progress indicators (e.g., 'Task progress: 0%', '40%', '100%') to show how the robot’s actions contribute to completing the overall goal. The visual style is consistent across all nodes, featuring a white robotic arm against a kitchen background with a microwave, ensuring clarity and focus on the task decomposition process. The overall structure emphasizes recursion and modularity, allowing the system to reason about long-term goals by breaking them into manageable, temporally compact subproblems."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents the overall framework of RadZero, divided into four main sections labeled (a), (b), (c), and (d), each describing a distinct component of the model's architecture. Section (a) illustrates the process of extracting finding-sentences from radiology reports using a Large Language Model (LLM). A sample radiology report is shown as input text, which is processed by the LLM to output a list of finding-sentences, such as 'There is left retrocardiac opacity' or 'There may be atelectasis'. These sentences are then used as targets for the vision model.

Section (b) details the core vision-language alignment mechanism. It begins with a radiology image, denoted as I_i, which is fed into a Vision Encoder (represented as a blue box with a snowflake icon). The encoded visual features are passed through Transformer Layers (pink box) to produce a Vision Token Sequence composed of L patches, visually represented as vertical pink bars. Simultaneously, a finding-sentence S_j^n is input into a Text Encoder (light green box), generating a Text Embedding. The vision token sequence and text embedding are then combined via two operations: first, a weighted sum (W-sum) operation, depicted as a horizontal arrow labeled 'w-sum', producing a Vision Embedding; second, a cosine similarity (cos-sim) computation, shown as a downward arrow labeled 'cos-sim', which yields Similarity Scores (orange squares). These scores are then used to compute the final Similarity Logit l_ij^n, represented as a yellow square.

Section (c) describes the training objective based on the similarity logit matrix. This matrix, labeled 'Similarity Logit Matrix', is structured as a grid where rows represent images I_1 to I_B and columns represent finding-sentences S_1^n to S_N^n. Each cell contains a '+' symbol indicating positive similarity, with some cells highlighted in yellow to emphasize selected pairs. Two losses are computed from this matrix: the InfoNCE Loss (L_T), indicated by a red arrow pointing right, and the MP-NCE Loss (L_I), indicated by a red arrow pointing down. The total loss L is defined as the sum of these two components: L = L_I + L_T.

Section (d) outlines the zero-shot inference pipeline. Starting from a single similarity probability l_ij^n (yellow square), it undergoes a sigmoid transformation σ to produce a normalized similarity score. This score is then used for Zero-Shot Classification (red arrow labeled 'Inference'). The process continues by constructing a Patch-Level Similarity Map M_ij^n from multiple patch-level similarities, followed by interpolation & σ to generate a Pixel-Level Similarity Map M_ij^n (visualized as a heatmap). Finally, this map is used for Zero-Shot Grounding and Zero-Shot Segmentation (red arrow labeled 'Inference'), enabling pixel-level localization of medical findings without requiring task-specific training data. The entire framework integrates vision, language, and contrastive learning to enable robust zero-shot performance in radiology interpretation."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates a comparative workflow between two segmentation prediction methods: Argmax-prob and RankSEG-RMA, presented side-by-side within a single horizontal layout. At the top, a header bar visually separates the two methods. On the left, 'Class A' is represented by a teal rectangle and 'Class B' by a salmon rectangle, indicating the two classes being segmented. An arrow labeled 'Argmax-prob' points from this header to a box labeled 'RankSEG-RMA', which then connects via a blue arrow to the final output stage. The main body of the diagram is vertically divided into two parallel processing paths: one for Argmax-prob (left side) and one for RankSEG-RMA (right side), both starting from the same input probability map. The input probability map, labeled 'Probability \\hat{p}', is a 4x4 grid of numerical values (e.g., 0.05, 0.48, 0.90, etc.) displayed in black text on a white background. From this grid, two branches emerge: one leading to a binary segmentation mask for Class A (teal square with a white cross) and another to a binary segmentation mask for Class B (salmon square with a salmon cross). These masks are labeled \\hat{\\mathcal{T}}_A^+ and \\hat{\\mathcal{T}}_B^+, respectively, with the superscript '+' indicating positive predictions. The Argmax-prob path continues from these masks to a final prediction image (teal cross on a teal background) labeled 'Argmax-prob Prediction (IoU^T = 61.69)', where IoU^T is shown in purple text. The RankSEG-RMA path, however, proceeds differently. After generating the initial masks \\hat{\\mathcal{T}}_A^+ and \\hat{\\mathcal{T}}_B^+, a step labeled '(1) Accept non-overlaps (2) Discard overlaps (3) Compute RMA-scores' is applied. This leads to a central RMA-scores matrix, which is a 4x4 grid of red numbers (e.g., 1.33, 2.09, 2.34) on a green background. Below this matrix, mathematical formulas define the overlap region \\hat{\\mathcal{T}}_{overlap} = \\hat{\\mathcal{T}}_A^+ \\cap \\hat{\\mathcal{T}}_B^+ and the resulting non-overlapping regions \\hat{\\mathcal{T}}_A = \\hat{\\mathcal{T}}_A^+ \\setminus \\hat{\\mathcal{T}}_{overlap} and \\hat{\\mathcal{T}}_B = \\hat{\\mathcal{T}}_B^+ \\setminus \\hat{\\mathcal{T}}_{overlap}. From the RMA-scores matrix, an arrow labeled \\delta_j = \\arg\\max_c \\hat{p}_{c,j} \\forall j \\in [d] points to the final RankSEG-RMA prediction image (salmon cross on a salmon background), labeled 'RankSEG-RMA Prediction (IoU^T = 63.04)' in blue text. The entire diagram uses color coding: teal for Class A, salmon for Class B, green for intermediate matrices, and white for background elements. Text labels are primarily black or colored to match their associated components. Arrows are solid lines with arrowheads indicating directionality, and some arrows are colored (purple for Argmax-prob, blue for RankSEG-RMA) to distinguish the two workflows. The diagram's purpose is to demonstrate that RankSEG-RMA achieves a higher IoU score (63.04) than Argmax-prob (61.69) by resolving overlaps using RMA-scores rather than simply selecting the class with the highest probability per pixel."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the ReAgent-V framework, a multi-agent reasoning system for video-based question answering. The layout is divided into four main horizontal sections: "Entropy-Calibrated Frame Selection" at the top, "Prediction Flow" in the middle-left, "Application" in the middle-right, and "Tool Factory" on the far left. The overall workflow progresses from top to bottom, starting with frame selection, moving to agent-based prediction, then application (inference/training), and finally leveraging feedback for tool refinement.

In the top section, "Entropy-Calibrated Frame Selection" is a purple-bordered box containing a question icon ("The sequence of color changes when the person picks up the paper in the video?") and a video clip sequence. Purple arrows point from the question to a series of video frames, which are then filtered and output as "Selected Video Frames." This section connects to the "Tool Factory" on the left via a dashed arrow, indicating tool invocation. The "Tool Factory" is a rounded rectangle with a toolbox icon, listing tools like OCR, Grounding DINO, ASR, Scene Graph, ShareGPT4Video, RAG Tool, and Clip, each represented by a distinct colored icon. These tools are used to process the selected video frames and generate context for the agents.

The central "Prediction Flow" section contains two main agents: the "Target Agent" (a robot icon with a toolbox) and the "Critic Agent" (a robot icon with a warning symbol). The Target Agent receives the selected video frames and tool context, generates an "Initial/Refined Answer" (represented by a gray circle labeled 'A'), and sends it to the Critic Agent. The Critic Agent evaluates this answer, producing either a green checkmark (acceptance) or red cross (rejection) along with "Critical Questions" (a question mark icon). If rejected, the Target Agent receives feedback via a "Reflection" loop (a magnifying glass icon) and revises its answer from three perspectives: "Conservative" (blue robot), "Neutral" (green robot), or "Aggressive" (red robot), each labeled with a corresponding color-coded icon. The final output is a "Final Answer" (black circle labeled 'A') and an "Evaluation Report" (a white box listing metrics like Accuracy, Fine-grained, etc.).

On the right side, the "Application" section is a vertical stack. The "Final Answer" flows via a dashed arrow to "Answer" (a blue rectangle), which then feeds into "Inference" (a 3D cube icon). Below this, "Training" (a gear icon) is connected to three algorithm boxes: "SFT," "DPO," and "GRPO" (all light blue rectangles). A dashed line from the "Evaluation Report" points to these training algorithms, indicating that high-scoring data from the feedback report is used to train these models. The entire diagram uses consistent visual cues: purple for selection processes, gray for intermediate steps, blue/green/red for agent strategies, and dashed lines for feedback loops and data flow."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents two main sections describing the RePIC framework: (a) the overall training framework and (b) the inference process in a skip-retrieval setting. In section (a), three distinct prompt templates are shown on the left, each with an associated visual example and question format. The first template, 'Object Consistency Template,' asks if an object named '<name>' from the first image appears in the second. The second, 'Visual Localization Template,' requests bounding box coordinates for a described region, such as 'horse on the right where only its rear half is visible.' The third, 'Identity Consistency Template,' asks for a detailed caption of the second image referencing the first image by name. These templates feed into a central training loop involving a 'Reference Model' and a 'Target Model.' A sample from the templates is passed to the Target Model, which is then evaluated against the Reference Model using a KL-div Loss metric. The output of the Target Model is compared against a 'Verifiable Reward' module, which computes rewards based on three criteria: Object Consistency (reward = 1 if correct, 0 if wrong), Visual Localization (reward = 1 if IoU ≥ 0.5, 0 otherwise), and Identity Consistency (reward = 1 if '<name>' appears in the output, 0 if not). The reward signal is then used to update the Target Model via GRPO Loss. Section (b) illustrates the inference phase. It begins with a 'Query Image' and a 'Database' containing multiple candidate images with names and descriptions. For instance, one entry is '<Baby_Q>: A baby is sleeping peacefully in a carrier...' and another is '<Bull_dog>: A French bulldog...'. The query image is processed through a Vision Encoder, which extracts features and feeds them into an LLM Decoder. The LLM Decoder generates a personal caption for the image, which is then compared against the database entries to retrieve relevant candidates. The entire inference pipeline is powered by an MLLM (Multimodal Large Language Model) that integrates vision and language processing. The bottom caption provides a narrative context for the example, describing the scene involving <Baby_Q> and <Bull_dog>."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates a multi-agent system for perturbation-driven image quality optimization, structured into three main horizontal sections: 'Generating Perturbation Models', 'Perceptual Analysis and Bidding', and 'Restoration Execution'. The top section, 'Generating Perturbation Models', begins with an 'Input Image' feeding into a 'Policy Model' which generates multiple 'Perturbed Models' by injecting Gaussian perturbations. These perturbed models produce restored images (I_p, I_1, ..., I_n), which are evaluated by 'Reward Models' to generate rewards (r_p, r_1, ..., r_n). These rewards pass through 'Reward Filters' to form a filtered subset S (f_1, f_2, ..., f_m), which is used in 'Group Computation' to calculate advantages (A_1, A_2, ..., A_m). This feedback loop updates parameters for 'Trained Models' and 'Frozen Models'. The middle section, 'Perceptual Analysis and Bidding', processes an input image via a 'Meta-Controller' that outputs weather conditions like 'Snow', 'Rain + Haze', or 'Snow + Haze'. These conditions trigger bid decisions based on historical success rates (TOP-1, TOP-2, TOP-3) from 'IQA Models'. The bottom section, 'Restoration Execution', shows the restoration process where the 'Meta-Controller' directs 'Perceptual Analysis and Bidding + Restoration Execution' to produce a 'Restored Image'. The restored image is then assessed for quality improvement using 'IQA Models', which determines if the restoration is 'Improved' or 'Not Improved'. If improved, the cycle continues; if not, it may lead to 'All Degradation Resolved. Congratulations!!!'. The entire system is interconnected with arrows indicating data flow and control signals, including feedback loops for parameter updates and iterative restoration cycles."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates the Z-Score Guided Early Stopping (ZGES) method for beam search, divided into two parts: (a) Beam Search and (b) Dynamic Step Quality Analysis. In part (a), a beam search process is depicted starting with an input x branching into multiple candidate paths represented by nodes labeled a1^1 through a4^4, each corresponding to a step in the search. These candidates are grouped into two stages: 'Peak' (highlighted in blue) and 'Stopping' (highlighted in red), indicating the transition point where quality begins to degrade. A black double-arrow symbolizes the continuation of beam search until the stopping condition is met, after which the output sequence y1 through y4 is generated. Above the beam search structure, a checkmark indicates successful completion, while a trash bin icon signifies discarded or terminated paths. Part (b) presents a graph titled 'Step Quality Mean' versus 'Step', showing a blue curve that rises to a peak and then declines, labeled 'Step Quality Degradation'. Two points on the curve are marked: one at the peak and another at the stopping point. An inset box highlights the 'Local Z-Score' calculation, displaying an orange bell-shaped curve with two data points annotated as λ=-0.6 and local z-score=-1.1, connected by a dashed red line. The horizontal axis represents 'Step', and the vertical axis represents 'Step Quality Mean'. The entire figure visually explains how the local z-score, computed cumulatively across steps, triggers early stopping when it drops below a threshold λ, allowing the LLM policy to generate the remainder of the trajectory from the peak step."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents a three-part schematic illustrating a dataset distillation framework designed to handle imbalanced data, progressing from conventional methods to an advanced adaptive soft-label calibration module. Part (a) shows the baseline approach: an 'Original Dataset' (depicted as stacked cylinders, color-coded green for balanced and red for long-tailed/real case) is fed into a single 'Distillation Model (DM)' (a neural network graph with blue nodes and black edges). This model generates 'Synthetic Images' (stacked red rectangles) and 'Soft-Labels' (yellow tags with numerical values like 0.2, 0.4, etc.), forming a 'Distilled Dataset'. A feedback loop labeled 'Feedback' connects the generated soft-labels back to the DM for iterative refinement. The 'Ideal Case' (green) and 'Real Case' (red) datasets are visually contrasted using bar charts above the input stack. Part (b) introduces a perturbation analysis framework using two specialized models: 'Distillation Model ①' (green network, for generating images) and 'Distillation Model ②' (red network, for generating labels). These models are trained on 'Balanced Datasets' (green cylinder) and 'Long-tailed Datasets' (red cylinder), respectively, indicated by arrows labeled 'Train'. The outputs — synthetic images and soft-labels — are combined via a '+' symbol to form a 'Distilled Dataset', which is visually represented as stacked red and green rectangles. Above this section, three configurations (Cfg_1, Cfg_2, Cfg_3) show different combinations of balanced and imbalanced datasets, each with distinct visual representations (e.g., Cfg_1 has all green, Cfg_3 has all red). An eye icon labeled 'Perturbation Analysis' suggests evaluating performance under varying imbalance levels. Part (c) details the 'Adaptive Soft-Label Alignment Module (ADSA)'. It begins with a 'Generate Δy' step (blue rounded rectangle), where Δy represents a smoothed adjustment factor derived from varying levels of imbalance. This Δy is applied to the original predicted logits (denoted by y̅, shown as stacked blue bars) through a 'Digitalize' process (downward arrow), producing a 'Balanced Soft-Label' output (grid of yellow boxes with adjusted probabilities like 0.3, 0.6, etc.). The final output is labeled 'Balanced Distilled Dataset'. The operator ⊕ (addition in logit space) is used to combine y̅ and Δy to produce ŷ (calibrated average confidence). The entire process aims to achieve uniformity in soft-label distributions across classes. Legend at the bottom defines symbols: green balance scale = Balanced Dataset, red bar chart = Imbalanced (Long-tailed) Dataset, red stacked rectangle = Synthetic Images, yellow tag = Soft-Label, and blue wavy line = Perturbation."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates a CNN-RNN hybrid architecture for spatiotemporal feature extraction from event data, organized into two parallel processing streams that converge toward detection heads. On the left, event data is represented as stacked 3D cubes filled with red dots, symbolizing event points across multiple time slices labeled E[t_{k-2}, t_{k-1}] and E[t_{k-n+1}, t_{k-n}]. Each cube stack is vertically aligned and connected via blue arrows indicating \"Backbone forward propagation\". These event tensors are individually processed by a \"to tensor\" module (blue rectangle), which converts them into standard tensor format, followed by a pink \"Head\" module. From there, each stream branches into a sequence of three identical blue rectangular modules labeled \"Decoupled Deformable-enhanced Recurrent Layer\", arranged horizontally in series. Each recurrent layer outputs two states: h_k^i and c_k^i (for i=1,2,3), shown as text labels above or below the layers. These layers are interconnected via downward-pointing blue arrows labeled \"Temporal information propagation\", indicating recurrence across time steps. Between each pair of recurrent layers, a green \"Downsample\" module is placed, reducing spatial resolution. The top stream's final output connects to a yellow \"Detection FPN+Head\" module, while the bottom stream's final output also connects to a separate yellow \"Detection FPN+Head\" module. Additionally, yellow arrows labeled \"Features fed into the detection head\" originate from the green downsample modules and point toward the respective detection heads, indicating multi-scale feature fusion. A legend at the bottom clarifies the arrow colors: blue for backbone forward propagation, pink for temporal information propagation, and yellow for features fed into the detection head. The overall structure emphasizes parallel processing of event sequences over different time windows, with recurrent layers capturing temporal dynamics and downsampling enabling multi-scale detection."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates a three-part methodology for training an Information Compression Module (ICM) using a large language model (LLM), structured into an overall pipeline (A), ICM architecture (B), and a three-stage training process (C). In section (A), the pipeline begins with a 'Query' image (e.g., a cat) and generates K candidate images (e.g., dog, panda) sharing the same artistic style but depicting different animals. Each image is compressed by the ICM into two tokens: t_con (content) and t_rel (relation). These compressed representations are fed into an LLM, which generates a reasoning string in the format '<think> ... <inspection-index-start> ... <inspection-index-end> ... </think> <answer> ... </answer>', enabling the model to reason about the relationship between the query and candidates. Section (B) details the ICM's internal structure: it takes an input image, processes it through two attention modules (ATT 1 and ATT 2), and outputs t_con and t_rel tokens. The first ATT 1 module extracts content features (e_con), while the second ATT 2 module combines these with relation features (R_q,c) to produce the final tokens. Section (C) outlines the three-stage training process. Stage 1 ('Self-Alignment for Pretraining ICM') uses a relationship prompt template and content prompt template to align the ICM’s output with ground-truth descriptions, optimizing via CE Loss. Stage 2 ('SFT with Synthetic Dataset for Activation') employs an Annotation MLLM to generate a synthetic CoT dataset, which is then used to fine-tune the LLM via SFT, improving retrieval and verification capabilities. Stage 3 ('RL with Curriculum Reward for Enhancement') leverages rollout-based reward calculation, evaluating outputs on Format, Result, and Efficiency metrics, and optimizing via GRPO to enhance performance. The entire process integrates visual reasoning, content compression, and reinforcement learning to train a robust ICM."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the MLLM-CD framework for lung cancer diagnosis, organized into three main horizontal sections: Contrastive Factor Discovery, Causal Structure Discovery, and Multimodal Counterfactual Reasoning, connected vertically by an Iterative Refinement loop. The top section begins with 'Multimodal Unstructured Data' on the left, containing two input boxes: one labeled 'Patient Info' with a patient profile ('62 y/o male smoker with ...') and another labeled 'Medical Image' showing chest X-rays. These inputs feed into a central 'Embeddings' block, depicted as a grid of blue and yellow squares, which undergoes 'Semantic Alignment'—indicated by curved orange arrows between the embedding grids. From this, two contrastive learning modules are shown: 'Intra-modal Contrastive' (with two chest X-ray images labeled Diagnosis A and B) and 'Inter-modal Contrastive' (showing a different patient profile and corresponding X-ray), both under the heading 'Contrastive Factor Discovery'. These modules output 'Factors' identified via an MLLM (represented as a blue robot icon) through 'Probe' and 'Annotation' interactions, leading to a 'Structured Data' box on the right—a table with columns for Gender, Smoking, and Lesion, each with binary values (1 or 0). This structured data feeds into the middle section, 'Causal Structure Discovery', where a 'Causal Discovery Algorithm' (symbolized by a bar chart and gears icon) generates a 'Causal Graph with Ambiguities', showing nodes for Gender, Smoking, Diagnosis, Age, and Lesion, with directed edges indicating causal relationships. The bottom section, 'Multimodal Counterfactual Reasoning', features a 'World Knowledge' module (blue robot icon) that poses the question 'What if the factor Lesion changed?' in a thought bubble. This triggers 'Counterfactual Generation', producing new 'Counterfactual Samples' (a patient profile and X-ray image) which are then subjected to 'Consistency Validation'. These counterfactuals loop back to the top-left 'Patient Info' and 'Medical Image' blocks via 'Iterative Refinement', forming a feedback loop. All components are enclosed in dashed rectangular boundaries, and arrows indicate directional flow from left to right and bottom to top. The entire process is designed to refine diagnostic models by identifying causal factors, discovering causal structures, and generating counterfactuals to resolve ambiguities."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates the MRGC framework, designed to mitigate increased classification complexity in condensed graphs caused by adversarial attacks. The top section shows a flow from an \"Original Graph (Attacked)\" on the left, through a central \"Graph Condensation\" process, to a \"Condensed Graph\" on the right. A red dashed arrow labeled \"Attacks\" points from the original graph to the condensation process, indicating that attacks (Feat. & Stru. & Label Attack) induce higher classification complexity, shown by an upward arrow labeled \"Classification Complexity ↑\" pointing from the original graph to the condensed graph. In response, the condensation process reduces complexity, indicated by a downward arrow labeled \"Classification Complexity ↓\" leading to the condensed graph. Below this main flow, three parallel modules are presented, each addressing a specific aspect of the problem: (1) Intrinsic Dimension Manifold Reg., (2) Curvature-Aware Manifold Smoothing, and (3) Class-Wise Manifold Decoupling. Each module is visually represented with diagrams, mathematical formulations, and arrows indicating the direction of effect. Module (1) shows a reduction in intrinsic dimension, depicted by a transition from a scattered point cloud to a lower-dimensional manifold, with formulas involving manifold volume and dimensionality. Module (2) focuses on smoothing class boundaries using curvature-aware regularization, illustrated with a mesh-like structure and equations defining curvature at structure and representation levels. Module (3) aims to decouple class manifolds, shown by separating overlapping manifold shapes, with a formula minimizing the difference between global manifold volumes. All three modules feed into the graph condensation process, working together to reduce classification complexity in the condensed graph."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the RobustMerge framework, which enhances model performance on seen tasks while enabling generalization to unseen tasks through parameter-efficient merging and inference. The layout is divided into three main sections labeled (a), (b), and (c), each enclosed in dashed boxes and connected by arrows indicating workflow progression. Section (a) 'Individual Training' shows separate training paths for seen tasks (e.g., Question answering, Captioning) and unseen tasks (e.g., Domain transfer, Math reasoning). Each task has its own weight matrix (ΔW₁ for captioning, ΔW_N for question answering) derived from a shared base model W, indicated by arrows pointing from W to each ΔW. These weights are color-coded: red for captioning-related components and blue for question answering-related components. Section (b) 'Parameter Efficient Merging' is subdivided into two steps: Step ① 'Pruning and Complementary Parameter Scaling' and Step ② 'Cross-Task Normalization'. In Step ①, the weight matrices (ΔW₁, ΔW_N) are processed through pruning and scaling operations, visualized as red and blue trapezoidal blocks with dots representing parameters. A circular arrow indicates iterative refinement, and annotations explain goals: 'maintain direction robustness' via low-rank space and 'mitigate gap from tail singular value'. Step ② shows cross-task normalization using stacked red and blue blocks, with arrows indicating alignment across tasks. A graph below depicts task-specific weight distributions, showing normalization across tasks. Section (c) 'Inference' demonstrates how the merged model W is deployed. It receives inputs from known (green) and unknown (red) task distributions, represented by bell curves and icons. The merged model W combines parameters A (red) and B (blue) from the previous steps, along with ΔW_m (a grid-like matrix), to produce outputs for both known and unknown tasks. Arrows indicate bidirectional flow between sections, emphasizing the iterative nature of the process. The entire diagram uses consistent color coding (red/blue for different tasks) and shapes (trapezoids, rectangles, grids) to represent different components and their relationships."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the S-GRPO (Serial Group Reinforcement Policy Optimization) framework, which contrasts two thought rollout strategies: Full Thought Rollout and Early-exit Thought Rollout, both feeding into a Serial group for reward computation and final advantage calculation. The layout is horizontally segmented into three main processing stages: initial policy generation, thought rollout with optional early exit, and serial group computation. A curved arrow at the top connects the end of the 'Early-exit Thought Rollout' back to the beginning of the 'Full Thought Rollout', indicating an iterative or feedback loop between the two modes. At the bottom, a legend defines symbols: O (Output), T (Thought), C (Conclusion), r (Reward), A (Advantage). 

On the far left, a 'Query' box initiates the process, feeding into a 'Policy model' (depicted as a beige rectangle with a flame icon) that generates the first output O^0. This output feeds into a vertical stack labeled 'Full Thought Rollout'. This stack contains n sequential thought steps T_1 through T_n, each enclosed in a rounded rectangle with a dashed red line indicating potential early termination. Each T_i step has an associated CoT^i (Chain-of-Thought) component shown as a colored oval with a '+' symbol and '<think>' tag. The CoT components are color-coded: green for T_1, orange for T_2, pink for T_n, suggesting different levels of confidence or stage-specific processing. The final thought T_n leads to a conclusion C^o, which is also connected to the output O^0 via a horizontal arrow. 

Parallel to the full rollout path, the 'Early-exit Thought Rollout' branch begins at the same O^0 output but diverges after T_1, allowing for early termination if a sufficient conclusion is reached. This branch includes a 'Policy model' (same beige rectangle with flame icon) that generates intermediate outputs O^1, O^2, ..., O^m, which are fed sequentially into a 'Decaying reward' module. This module is a light blue rectangle containing the formula r = 1 / 2^(N_high - 1), where N_high is the number of high-level thoughts considered, and it features a checkmark and cross symbol indicating successful or failed reward conditions. The computed rewards r^1, r^2, ..., r^m are then passed to a 'Group computation' box, which aggregates them into advantages A^1, A^2, ..., A^m. These advantages are then processed further, as indicated by ellipses leading to A^0, implying a final aggregation or normalization step. The entire 'Serial group' section is enclosed within a gray shaded area labeled 'Serial group'."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents SAEMARK, a watermarking system for LLM-generated text, divided into two main vertical sections: \"Watermark Generation\" on the left and \"Watermark Detection\" on the right, both connected via shared components. The top section, \"Watermark Generation,\" begins with \"① Target Feature Sequence Generation\": a rounded rectangle labeled \"User Information\" (e.g., \"User-12345\") flows via an arrow labeled \"Hashing\" to another rounded rectangle labeled \"Watermark Key\" (e.g., \"0c42f1a\"). This key then flows via an arrow labeled \"Generate Targets\" to a box titled \"Target Sequence\" listing example sentences with numerical scores (e.g., \"Sentence #1: 0.172\"). Below this is \"② Rejection Sampling\": a box labeled \"LLM API\" with a cube icon receives a prompt (\"Let’s talk about time traveling.\") and generates text fragments. These fragments are processed by a \"Feature Extractor\" (icon: bar chart), which outputs scores; one score (0.268) is marked with a red X, while another (0.156) is marked with a green check and labeled \"Closest to Target.\" A dotted arrow loops from the output back to the LLM API prompt, indicating iterative refinement. The bottom section, \"Feature Extractor: Sparse Auto-encoder,\" details the internal mechanism: an \"Input\" (e.g., \"It remains pure theoretical.\") flows to an \"Anchor LLM\" (Gemma-2B, Layer 20, Residual Stream) which outputs \"Activations\" ([0.1, 0.0, ..., 0.0]). These activations feed into a \"Sparse Auto-encoder\" (Gemma Scope) producing a \"Sparse Feature Distribution\" and a final \"Feature Concentration Score\" (e.g., 0.156). This component is noted as replaceable by any deterministic feature extractor yielding a Gaussian distribution. The right section, \"Watermark Detection,\" mirrors the generation process: \"① Target Seq. Generation\" uses the same \"Watermark Key\" to generate the \"Target Sequence\". \"② Feat. Calculation\" sends the target sequence to a \"Feature Extractor\" (same icon as in generation) which computes \"Scores\" for candidate texts. Two example texts are shown: one matching the target (score 0.156) and one not (score 0.421). Finally, \"③ Validation\" compares these scores to determine if the text is \"Watermarked by key: 0c42f1a\" or \"Not Watermarked.\" The entire diagram uses consistent visual elements: rounded rectangles for data/keys, boxes for processes, arrows for flow, and icons for specific modules. Text labels include numerical values, color-coded indicators (red X/green check), and explanatory notes such as \"No Training or Logits Involved. Works with open and proprietary LLMs.\""
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates the two-stage architecture of SAVVY, a system for constructing a global map from an egocentric video and spatial audio input. The layout is horizontally divided into two main sections labeled 'Stage 1: Egocentric Spatial Track Construction' on the left and 'Stage 2: Global Map Construction' on the right. At the top left, an 'Input: Question' block contains a sequence of four video frames showing a room scene, with a temporal grounding bar indicating timestamps from 0:05 to 0:08. Below this, three parallel processing branches are initiated by text queries targeting specific objects in the video: 'A gas stovetop with multiple burners' (marked with a green triangle), 'A built-in refrigerator with light-colored panels' (marked with a purple square), and 'A woman with long dark hair, wearing a yellow vest' (marked with a yellow circle). Each branch leads to a corresponding module: (a) Snapshot Descriptor, (b) Text-guided Snapshot Segmentation, and (c) Spatial Audio Cues. Module (a) displays a bounding box around the object with fields for timestamp, direction, and distance. Module (b) shows a segmented image of the object with similar metadata fields. Module (c) presents a directional-of-arrival diagram with axes X, Y, Z, angles φt and θ, and a formula for distance dt = √(K * CDR), where CDR stands for Coherent-to-Diffuse Ratio. These three modules feed into a central 'SRP-PHAT' block, which processes the audio cues. On the right side, under Stage 2, the output of SRP-PHAT feeds into 'Egocentric Track' visualization, which includes a camera icon, angle θ, and radius r. This track is then processed through 'Location Clustering' for static objects (purple cluster for reference object, green cluster for facing object) and 'Dynamic Track Interpolate & smooth' for moving objects, indicated by colored dots with timestamps (e.g., 0.08s, 0.07s) and legend markers for SD Track, Seg Track, and Audio Track. A color-coded legend at the top right defines object types: purple for static reference, green for static facing, and yellow for dynamic target. The entire diagram uses a clean white background with black lines and colored shapes to differentiate components."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates the SPINT architecture, a neural decoding model designed to predict behavior covariates at the last timestep using a past window of neural activity from an unordered set of neurons. The layout is divided into two main horizontal sections: the top section handles neural identity inference via a 'Neural ID encoder' trained on M-shot calibration trials, and the bottom section combines this identity information with neural activity to produce a behavior prediction through a cross-attention mechanism. The entire process flows left-to-right, with clear directional arrows indicating data flow.

In the top section, on the far left, 'M-shot neural calibration trials' are represented as three stacked horizontal layers of rectangular blocks—yellow (top), green (middle), and blue (bottom)—each containing multiple blocks arranged in rows, labeled 'N' vertically to indicate neuron count per layer. These trials feed into a 'Neural ID encoder', enclosed in a light blue rounded rectangle. Inside this encoder, there are two parallel vertical stacks of gray rectangular nodes connected by bidirectional arrows, representing a neural network structure. A central vertical label reads 'Trial-wise pooling', indicating the pooling operation performed across trials. The output of this encoder is directed downward to the bottom section.

In the bottom section, the input is labeled 'Window' and consists of three horizontal layers of colored rectangular blocks—yellow, green, and blue—each containing multiple blocks arranged in rows, again labeled 'N' vertically to denote neuron count. This represents 'Unordered neural activity'. To the right of this, a '+' symbol indicates addition or combination with another component: 'Context-dependent neural ID', also enclosed in a light blue rounded rectangle. This component contains three horizontal layers of striped rectangular blocks—yellow with diagonal stripes, green with horizontal stripes, and blue with vertical stripes—each containing multiple blocks. These represent the 'ID-informed neural activity' derived from the neural ID encoder's output. The combined output from these two components feeds into a red-bordered rounded rectangle labeled 'Cross-attention'. Within this block, the inputs are labeled 'K' (key), 'V' (value), and 'Q' (query), indicating the standard transformer attention components. The 'Q' is sourced from 'Learnable behavior queries', shown as a vertical stack of purple and green rectangular blocks labeled 'B', which are fed into the cross-attention module. The output of the cross-attention module is then directed to the right into a dashed-outlined box labeled 'Behavior covariates', which contains a single purple block and a single green block, representing the predicted behavior covariates. The final output is labeled 'Last step prediction'.

The figure includes a small coordinate system in the lower-left corner, with axes labeled 'neurons' (vertical) and 'time' (horizontal), with 'trials' marked along the time axis, indicating that the neural activity is sampled over time and across neurons. The overall structure emphasizes the separation of identity inference from activity decoding, followed by selective aggregation via cross-attention to produce a behavior prediction."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents the SRA-CL framework, divided into two main sections: \"Intra-User Contrastive Learning via Item Semantic Retrieval\" (top) and \"Inter-User Contrastive Learning via Item Semantic Retrieval\" (bottom), each illustrating a distinct learning paradigm. The top section details intra-user contrastive learning, where user-item interactions are processed to learn user semantic embeddings. User profiles (represented by icons of different users) are input into an LLM (depicted as a neural network icon), which generates user semantic embeddings (color-coded horizontal bars). These embeddings are stored in a database. A synthesized positive sample is created using a weighted sum of retrieved item embeddings, and a contrastive loss function (Lcs) is applied to align the user embedding with the positive sample while pushing away negative samples. The bottom section illustrates inter-user contrastive learning, where item semantic embeddings are generated from item descriptions (name, brand, category, description) using an LLM. These embeddings are also stored in a database. Two separate sequence encoders process user sequences and item sequences, respectively. A contrastive loss (Lis) is applied between user and item embeddings to learn cross-modal representations. Both sections include retrieval mechanisms, attention modules, and adapters for fine-tuning. A legend on the right indicates trainable components (marked with a flame icon) and frozen components (marked with a snowflake icon). The overall layout is structured to show the flow of data from user/item inputs through encoding, retrieval, and contrastive learning stages, culminating in improved semantic understanding for personalized recommendation."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates a multi-stage image super-resolution pipeline integrating text prompts and spatially constrained diffusion modeling. The global layout is a left-to-right flow starting from a low-resolution (LR) input image, branching into two parallel processing paths before converging into a central diffusion network, followed by post-processing modules leading to a final super-resolved (SR) output. The top-left branch processes the LR image through a 'DAPE' module, which extracts semantic text tags such as 'beak', 'bird', 'black', 'head', 'penguin', 'stone', 'stand', 'yellow'. These tags are displayed in green text below the DAPE box. Simultaneously, the LR image is fed into a 'Grounded SAM' module, which generates a set of five visually grounded tag-mask pairs — each tag ('beak', 'head', 'penguin', 'stone', 'yellow') is paired with a corresponding segmentation mask — shown as small images above the 'Spatially Re-Focused Cross Attention (SRCA)' block. An 'Ungrounded Mask' — depicted as a black silhouette with white outline — is derived as the complement of all grounded masks and flows independently to the right. The SRCA block, colored orange, receives inputs from both the DAPE-extracted tags and the grounded masks, and feeds them into the diffusion network. The diffusion network is represented as a wide, blue, hourglass-shaped structure labeled 'Diffusion Network', containing multiple 'CA Layer' blocks (each a light beige rectangle) arranged symmetrically on both sides, with ellipses indicating intermediate layers. The diffusion process progresses from noise level ε_t to ε_{t-1} via these CA layers, which integrate text-conditioning signals spatially guided by the masks. The output ε_{t-1} is then passed to an orange 'STCFG' block, which applies Classifier-Free Guidance (CFG) differentially: it uses the grounded mask to condition CFG on relevant regions while applying unconditional guidance to ungrounded regions. The result, ε'_{t-1}, is fed into a circular node labeled 'T', representing T denoising steps. Finally, the refined latent representation is decoded by an 'Image Decoder' (light beige box) to produce the final SR image, shown as a high-resolution photograph of a penguin. Arrows indicate data flow: solid arrows show direct computation or transformation, while dashed arrows denote optional or auxiliary pathways. The entire diagram is structured to emphasize the integration of text prompts and spatial grounding within a diffusion-based generative framework."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates the STITCH-OPE method, divided into five labeled sections (A-E), arranged in a top-to-bottom, left-to-right flow. Section A shows two square grids side-by-side: the left grid labeled 'Behavior Data' contains a dense set of colored dots connected by curved lines, representing observed trajectories; the right grid labeled 'Partial Rollouts' shows a similar but sparser pattern, indicating truncated trajectories. An arrow points from Behavior Data to Partial Rollouts, and another arrow descends to section C. Section B, titled 'Pre-Trained Diffusion', contains three main components: on the left, a box labeled 'State + Noise' showing a point near a cluster of dots with input notation (st, ε^k); in the center, a horizontal sequence of three circular nodes labeled τ^k → ... → τ^0, representing the backward diffusion process; on the right, a box labeled 'Partial Rollout' showing a single curved line with three black dots along it, leading to an output labeled 'Target Rollouts'. An arrow connects State + Noise to the diffusion sequence, which connects to Partial Rollout, and then to Target Rollouts. Below this section, a dashed box labeled 'Policy Guidance' contains two square grids: the left grid shows concentric dashed arcs radiating outward, labeled with ∇_τ Σ_t log π(a_t|s_t); the right grid shows a grid of colored dots connected by lines, labeled with ∇_τ Σ_t log β(a_t|s_t). A minus sign separates these two grids. An upward arrow from Policy Guidance points to Pre-Trained Diffusion. Section C, labeled 'Reward Predictor', contains a rectangular box with inputs s_t and a_t, and output r_t, with the internal formula Ŝ(s_t, a_t) → r_t. The box is labeled 'Reward Predictor'. An arrow points from Behavior Data to this box. Section D is positioned below C and contains the same two grids as in Policy Guidance, with the same labels and minus sign, and is labeled 'Policy Guidance'. An arrow points from this section upward to Pre-Trained Diffusion. Section E, located to the right of B, contains the 'Target Rollouts' box from B, and below it, a formula labeled 'Off-Policy Evaluation': Ĵ(π) = E_{p_w^π}[Σ_t γ^t Ŝ(s_t, a_t)]. An arrow points from Target Rollouts to this formula. All sections are enclosed in dashed boxes. Orange highlights indicate novel contributions: 'Partial Rollouts', 'Target Rollouts', 'Reward Predictor', 'Policy Guidance', and 'Off-Policy Evaluation'."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates a two-part deep learning architecture for generating human-object interaction (HOI) videos with synchronized motion. The top section is labeled 'Synchronized Video-Motion Diffusion (SViMo)' and the bottom section is 'Vision-aware 3D Interaction Diffusion (VID)', connected by feedback loops. The entire system is designed for joint generation of video and motion, followed by 3D interaction reconstruction using the generated output.

In the SViMo module, inputs include a sample timestep t, sinusoidal position encoding (PE), prompts P (e.g., '<spatula, scrape, plate>'), and a reference image I. These feed into a T5 Encoder to produce e_text. Simultaneously, the reference image passes through two 3D VAE encoders to generate latent vectors z_I^V and z_I^M. These are combined with e_text via addition operations to form e_V and e_M. All three latent representations (e_text, e_V, e_M) are fed into a central 'Triple Modality Adaptive Modulation' block, which contains multiple parallel modules: '3D Full Attention', 'Point-wise Feedforward', and 'Gate' layers, each with 'Scale & Shift' (S&S) components. The modulated features f_text, f_V, f_M are concatenated and passed through a sequence of layers represented by vertical bars (green, purple, blue) before being projected into latent codes z_V^0 and z_M^0. These are decoded by two 3D VAE decoders to produce the final rendered video M and motion video P. A loss function L_SViMo is defined as the sum of squared reconstruction errors between predicted and ground-truth latent codes. An orange arrow labeled 'Gradient Constrain' indicates gradient backpropagation from the decoder to the encoder.

The VID module takes the rendered motion video M as input. It first undergoes a 'Projection' step to generate a 'Generated 3D Interaction' (h_t, o_t). This is processed by a 'Cross Attention' module within a 'Denoising UNet' structure, which also receives inputs from a 'Fusion' layer combining multiscale 3D convolutions. The output is a refined 3D interaction representation. A training loss L_VID is defined as the sum of squared error between predicted and target interaction states plus a chamfer distance term. The 3D interaction output is then fed back into the SViMo module via an orange dashed arrow labeled 'Grad. Backpropagate', forming a closed-loop optimization mechanism. The diagram includes a legend on the right explaining symbols: '+' for channel-wise concatenation, orange dashed arrows for gradient backpropagation, gray cubes for diffused latent codes, red vertical bars for multiscale 3D convolutions, purple boxes for S&S modules, and green boxes for training loss functions."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents the SYMPHONY system architecture, illustrating a multi-agent reinforcement learning framework for e-commerce product search. The layout is vertically structured into three main horizontal sections: top (Current Observation), middle (Agent Pool & Learning Dynamics), and bottom (WebShop Interface & Memory Module). The top section shows user interaction with a product page (microphone listing) and agent actions (clicking 'Prev', 'Buy Now', or 'Description'), each leading to different outcomes (failure or pending task). A reward function R(s_{t+1}) = Z(s_t) * (1 - E(s_t)) is shown, where Z represents success probability and E represents error rate, with three reward levels r1, r2, r3 corresponding to different facial expressions. The middle section features a heterogeneous agent pool (illustrated with musicians playing various instruments) that generates diverse search strategies. These agents interact with a state transition graph showing states S1 through S6 across two agents (Agent1 and Agent2), with arrows indicating transitions, some marked with red Xs (failure) or green checkmarks (success). A dotted arrow labeled 'Update' points from the agent pool to the state graph, suggesting iterative learning. The bottom section depicts a webshop interface where an instruction ('I want a noise cancelling...') triggers a search, displaying product results (headphones, microphones) with prices. A memory module on the right stores past failures, with a circular 'UPDATE' arrow indicating continuous learning. The entire diagram uses icons (musicians, microphones, headphones) and color-coded states (red for failure, green for success) to visually represent the system's decision-making process."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates a comparative workflow between lookahead reasoning and autoregressive decoding over time, highlighting efficiency gains. The global layout is divided into two main vertical sections: the left side shows the 'Draft' and 'Target' processes stacked vertically, while the right side contains a legend defining 'Prefix', 'Draft Step', and 'Target Step'. Below these, two horizontal timelines represent 'Lookahead Reasoning' and 'Autoregressive Decoding', both plotted against a 'Time' axis marked at intervals 0, t, 2t, 2t+T, and 3T. The top section, labeled 'Draft', displays three parallel sequences initiated by a 'Prefix' block, each generating a draft token: s1-hat, s2-hat, s3-hat, shown as light blue rectangles. The 'Target' section below it mirrors this structure, showing target tokens s1, s2, s3 in pink rectangles, each preceded by a 'Prefix' block. Vertical arrows connect draft tokens to corresponding target tokens, indicating verification. Subscripts indicate equivalence: s1 ≈ s1-hat, s2 ≈ s2-hat, but s3 ≠ s3-hat. The right-side legend clarifies that 'Prefix' is gray, 'Draft Step' is light blue, and 'Target Step' is pink. A dashed red arrow labeled 'speedup' points from the lookahead reasoning output to the autoregressive decoding timeline, illustrating the time savings. The lookahead reasoning row shows the draft tokens s1-hat, s2-hat, s3 (pink) being output early, followed by the corrected s3 from the target. The autoregressive decoding row shows sequential generation of s1, s2-prime, s3-prime, each taking one full target step T. The caption explains that the draft model proposes γ=3 steps, the target generates corresponding tokens, and a verifier checks semantic equivalence. If the first two steps match but the third does not, lookahead reasoning outputs the verified draft steps (s1-hat, s2-hat) followed by the corrected s3, achieving a total time of 2t+T versus 3T for autoregressive decoding. The figure uses color coding (light blue for draft, pink for target), shapes (rectangles for tokens), and text labels to convey the process flow and timing differences."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates the SCENEWEAVER pipeline, a system designed to iteratively refine 3D scenes using a reason–act–reflect paradigm. The overall layout is divided into three main vertical sections on the left (Initializer, Implementer, Refiner), a central control module (Planner and Executor), and two output sections on the right (Final Scene and Assets). A central 'SceneWeaver' spider icon symbolizes the orchestrator. The process begins with an initial query, flows through planning and execution phases, and culminates in a final 3D scene. The pipeline is cyclical, incorporating feedback loops for iterative refinement.

On the left, the 'Initializer' section (yellow background) presents two tools: Tool #1 MetaScenes and Tool #2 PhyScene, shown with corresponding 3D scene previews. Below it, the 'Implementer' section (yellow background) features Tool #1 ACDC and Tool #2 LLM, each with visual examples of furniture placement. The 'Refiner' section (yellow background) at the bottom includes three tools: Tool #1 VLM (Update Rotation), Tool #2 Rule (Add Relation), and Tool #3 LLM (Remove Object), each accompanied by illustrative diagrams showing object manipulation or removal. These tools are visually represented as 3D objects with annotations.

In the center, the 'Planner' module (pink background) contains four rectangular nodes arranged in a loop: 'Think', 'Reflection', 'Action', and 'Feedback'. Arrows indicate a cyclic flow: 'Think' leads to 'Action', which feeds into 'Feedback', which then connects back to 'Reflection', which loops to 'Think'. An arrow from the 'Query' input points to 'Think'. A green 'STOP' button with a checkmark indicates termination conditions. Below the Planner is the 'Executor' module (blue background), labeled '+Physical optimize', containing three icons representing physical constraints: 'Collision' (a dashed box with arrows), 'Out of bound' (an arrow pointing left/right), and 'Relation' (a table with a person icon). The Executor receives input from 'Action' and outputs to the 'Assets' section.

On the right, the 'Final Scene' (top-right, beige 3D room view) displays a completed interior with multiple tables, chairs, and cabinets. Below it, the 'Assets' section (orange background) lists available 3D assets categorized under '3D FUTURE', 'MetaScene', 'Objaverse', and 'Infinitigen', each with small 3D model thumbnails. A dashed line connects the Executor to the Assets, indicating asset selection or utilization. The entire diagram uses color-coded sections (yellow, pink, blue, orange) and clear directional arrows to convey the workflow. The central 'SceneWeaver' spider icon, surrounded by tools and arrows, emphasizes its role as the system’s core coordinator."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents a multi-stage pipeline for low-light image enhancement and 3D illumination-driven projection, labeled as LIAR. The overall layout is divided into three main horizontal sections: \"Selective Low-light Image Enhancement\" (top), \"2D Illumination-guided Sampling\" (middle), and \"3D Illumination-driven Projection\" (bottom), each with distinct color backgrounds (light green, light orange, light blue respectively) and interconnected components. A top-level flowchart at the very top shows the complete end-to-end process starting from input X, passing through SLIE, 2D Encoder, 2D-IGS, 3D-IDP, and Predictor to produce a final 3D occupancy map visualization. A legend on the top right defines symbols used throughout the diagram: Element-wise Division (circle with division symbol), Element-wise Multiplication (circle with multiplication symbol), BEV Pooling (circle with plus sign), Add (circle with plus sign), Affected Grid (red grid), Refined Grid (blue grid), Projected Point (red dot), and Sample Point (blue triangle). In the \"Selective Low-light Image Enhancement\" section, input X enters a Retinex Decomposition block which outputs I. This I is then evaluated against a threshold λ(I) derived from a summation over pixel intensities and variance; if λ(I) > t, output is X, otherwise it's X ⊙ I (element-wise multiplication). A visual representation shows night scenes (purple dots) versus well-lit day scenes (yellow triangles) mapped onto a grid. The \"2D Illumination-guided Sampling\" section takes input I and passes it through a guidance network Φ_G to generate guidance g (a heatmap with values like 0.3, 0.7) and offsets Δp and weights Δw. These guide a Feature Sampling block to produce F_img, which is then passed to the 3D-IDP module. The \"3D Illumination-driven Projection\" section begins with a camera capturing image I, which is projected onto a 3D grid to form an Illumination Intensity Field S. From this, a Context Feature F_cx is extracted using a depth D and a projection operator P. This context feature is combined with a Residual Query Q' via an Aggregation operation to form a Context Query Q. Both Q and Q' feed into a fusion unit producing F_bev, which is then passed to the predictor. The entire pipeline integrates 2D and 3D processing to handle illumination deficiencies in different regions of the scene, enhancing features in underexposed areas and compensating for semantic loss in overexposed areas before final occupancy prediction."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates a semi-supervised learning framework using knowledge distillation between a Teacher SAM and a Student SAM. The global layout is vertically divided into two main horizontal blocks: the top 'Teacher SAM' block (light blue background) and the bottom 'Student SAM' block (light orange background), both processing image inputs to produce mask outputs. On the left, 'Batch data' is split into labeled data (x^l) and unlabeled data (x^u), feeding into respective encoders. The Teacher SAM's components — an 'Original encoder' (blue trapezoid) and 'Mask decoder' (blue trapezoid) — are marked as 'Frozen' (blue snowflake icon). The Student SAM's components — an 'Efficient encoder' (orange trapezoid) and 'Mask decoder' (orange trapezoid) — are marked as 'Learnable' (orange flame icon). A vertical 'Image embedding' pathway runs through both networks, represented by stacked gray rectangles. The Teacher SAM produces output mask predictions p_T^u for unlabeled data and p_T^l for labeled data. The Student SAM produces corresponding outputs p_S^u and p_S^l. The Teacher's outputs are connected to the Student's outputs via two distillation pathways: 'Embedding distillation' (blue arrows pointing downward from Teacher's embedding to Student's embedding, labeled 'DLW') and 'Output distillation' (blue arrows pointing from Teacher's mask outputs to Student's mask outputs). Labeled data (x^l) receives direct supervision from 'Ground Truth' (black mask icons) via red arrows labeled 'Label supervision'. Unlabeled data (x^l) receives supervision only through the distillation pathways. A legend in the top-right corner indicates red arrows represent 'Label supervision' and blue arrows represent 'Knowledge distillation'. The 'DLW' module (teal rectangle) sits between the embedding layers and is connected to both the Teacher and Student embeddings, suggesting it modulates the distillation strength. The diagram uses color coding (blue for Teacher, orange for Student) and icons (snowflake for frozen, flame for learnable) to distinguish components. Text labels identify each component and process, including 'Image embedding', 'Mask decoder', 'Output distillation', and 'Ground Truth'."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents a three-stage workflow for a self-evolving learning framework, visually organized into three vertically stacked, color-coded sections labeled ①, ②, and ③, each representing a distinct phase. Section ①, titled 'Semantic-Guided Pseudo Input Generation' and set against a pale yellow background, shows an input symbol (an orange speech bubble with a question mark, labeled D^i) flowing via a solid black arrow into a central atomic structure icon labeled LLM^0. This icon represents a foundational large language model, depicted with a gray atom-like design featuring pink dots. A second solid black arrow then leads from LLM^0 to an output symbol (another orange speech bubble with a question mark, labeled X_p^i), indicating the generation of pseudo-inputs. Section ②, titled 'Label Self-Evolution' and set against a pale green background, begins with the pseudo-input X_p^i (same orange speech bubble icon) entering a dashed rectangular boundary. Inside this boundary, two identical atomic structure icons are shown: the top one is labeled LLM^i (representing a current or evolving model), and the bottom one is labeled LLM^0 (the same foundational model from section ①). Both LLM^i and LLM^0 are connected to a small green magnifying glass icon and a small blue snowflake icon, suggesting search or refinement operations. A solid black arrow exits the dashed boundary, pointing to an output symbol (orange speech bubble with a question mark) labeled D_p^i(X_p^i, Y_p^i), signifying the creation of pseudo-samples with refined labels. Section ③, titled 'Rehearsal with Similarity Regularization' and set against a light gray background, contains a dashed rectangular box on the left side. This box contains two elements: the pseudo-sample dataset D_p^i(X_p^i, Y_p^i) (orange speech bubble icon) and a historical pseudo-sample dataset D_p^{1...i-1} (also an orange speech bubble icon, but with a green magnifying glass icon next to it). These two datasets feed into a central atomic structure icon labeled LLM^i, which is also accompanied by a green magnifying glass icon. A solid black arrow points from LLM^i to another atomic structure icon labeled LLM^{i+1}, which is accompanied by a blue calculator icon with mathematical symbols (+, -, =, ÷), indicating a computational or fine-tuning step. Above the LLM^i node, a downward-pointing arrow labeled L_reg originates from a node labeled 'Task Similarity W', which itself receives input from the two datasets within the dashed box. This indicates that the regularization term L_reg is computed based on task similarity and applied during the fine-tuning process. Below the LLM^i node, a solid black arrow points to the LLM^{i+1} node, showing the progression of the model through the rehearsal stage. The final output of this stage is represented by the LLM^{i+1} node, which incorporates both the rehearsal samples and the new training data D^{i+1} (a blue calculator icon) for fine-tuning."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates the SPAGD framework, divided into two main horizontal sections: an upper reconstruction-based anomaly detection pipeline and a lower spatiotemporal modeling module. The top section begins with 'Time-Series Data' represented by green and orange wavy lines, feeding into a light blue 'Encoder' box, then a peach-colored 'Decoder' box, producing 'Reconstructed Time-Series' (green and orange wavy lines with red error markers). A downward arrow from the input data leads to a yellow 'Graph Construction' block, generating a 'Relation Graph' depicted as a small network of interconnected nodes (green, blue, orange, gray circles). This relation graph is then passed to a yellow 'Anomaly Aware Module', which outputs an 'Adjusted Relation Graph' where one node is highlighted in red, indicating anomaly detection. The bottom section shows two parallel inputs: 'Normal Time-series' (blue background) and 'Perturbed Time-series' (pink background), each with corresponding graphs. These feed into a pink dashed-bordered 'Spatial Convolution' block, which performs 'Self loop' and 'Neighbor Aggregation' operations on the graph nodes, indicated by arrows and dotted circles. The output of spatial convolution feeds into a gray dashed-bordered 'Temporal Convolution' block, which processes 'C Temporal Chunks' (stacked layers of graphs). This result is passed to a light green 'Predictor' block, which outputs a final anomaly score denoted by 'p-hat' inside a green square labeled 'Anomaly Prediction'. Arrows throughout the diagram indicate the directional flow of data and processing steps."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the DISCOVR framework, which processes an input video through two parallel branches — a video branch and an image branch — to extract and align spatio-temporal features via semantic cluster distillation. The global layout is divided into three main sections: left (video processing), center (shared encoder and loss module), and right (semantic cluster distillation). The video branch on the left takes a sequence of 4 grayscale video frames stacked vertically, labeled 'Time' along the horizontal axis, indicating temporal progression. These frames are tokenized into 3D patches, represented as a vertical stack of small cubes, which feed into a blue rectangular block labeled 'Video Encoder'. A dashed arrow labeled 'Gradients' points from this encoder to a green oval labeled 'L_vid_ssl', indicating a masked self-supervised loss. The output of the video encoder is passed to a blue rectangle labeled 'Decoder', which reconstructs the masked video tokens. A dashed arrow labeled 'Gradients' also connects the decoder to the same 'L_vid_ssl' loss. Below the video branch, the image branch processes the same video frames but extracts 2D patches per frame, shown as a grid of colored squares (orange and green) arranged horizontally with 'Time' indicated above, representing frame-by-frame extraction. These 2D patches feed into a green rectangular block labeled 'Image Encoder'. A dashed arrow labeled 'Gradients' points from this encoder to a green oval labeled 'L_img_ssl', another masked self-supervised loss. The output of the image encoder is connected via a solid black arrow to a white rectangular box labeled 'L_SCD', which represents the semantic cluster distillation loss. This box receives inputs from both the video decoder (via a dashed arrow labeled 'Gradients') and the image encoder (via a solid arrow). The right section, titled 'Semantic Cluster Distillation', shows the alignment process. A vertical stack of light-blue boxes labeled 'Temporal Features' flows into a mathematical expression H(s_v, q_i), where s_v and q_i are variables. Above this expression is a label 'L_SCD', indicating the loss function. Below, a grid of green squares labeled 'Online Fine-grained Spatial Features' (with time index t=1...t=N) feeds into a yellow rectangular box labeled 'Sinkhorn Targets'. A solid arrow labeled 'Sinkhorn Clustering' points from the spatial features to the Sinkhorn Targets. A dashed arrow labeled 'Gradients' points from the Sinkhorn Targets back to the H(s_v, q_i) expression, completing the feedback loop. The entire diagram uses color-coded blocks (blue for video, green for image, white for loss, yellow for targets) and arrows (solid for data flow, dashed for gradients) to distinguish components and relationships."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents a three-stage pipeline for the SemCoT model, divided into two main phases: training (Step I and Step II) and inference (Step III), each enclosed in dashed boxes labeled (a) Training stage and (b) Inference stage. The overall structure flows vertically from bottom to top, representing data processing through layers, with horizontal arrows indicating cross-component interactions or dependencies.

In Step I (Training stage, left panel), the process begins with "Semantic embeddings" at the top, which feed into a "Pooling & Linear project" layer (pink box with fire icon, indicating trainable). Below this is a "Middle five transformer layers" block (also pink with fire icon), followed by "LLM Tokenization" (pink box). These are grounded by two green boxes at the bottom: "Condensed reasoning" and "Ground-truth reasoning". An arrow labeled L_sim connects the semantic embeddings to a comparison mechanism above the pooling layer, suggesting similarity computation. This entire section trains a "Custom. sent. trans." module (pink box with snowflake icon, indicating frozen), which receives input from the tokenized query and processes it via a "Linear layer" (pink with fire icon) before outputting to the LLM. A dotted red box highlights the "Implicit reasoning generator" component, which takes "Input query" and multiple "<cot>" tokens as inputs, feeding into a "Lightweight LM (distilled/sheared from LLM)" (pink box with fire icon). This lightweight LM outputs to the LLM, which then generates the final answer (Ans.).

In Step II (Training stage, middle panel), the focus shifts to training the "Implicit reasoning generator". It includes a "Tokenize" step feeding into a "Linear layer" (pink with fire icon), which connects to the "Lightweight LM". The "Ground-truth reasoning" component provides ground-truth reasoning data to train the lightweight LM. The "Custom. sent. trans." module is shown again, now receiving input from the lightweight LM. The LLM receives input from both the "Custom. sent. trans." and the "Implicit reasoning generator". The "Ground-truth ans." and "Pred Ans." are compared using an L_sem metric, which feeds back into the "Custom. sent. trans." for refinement. The "Implicit reasoning generator" is also connected to the "Lightweight LM" via a feedback loop.

In Step III (Inference stage, right panel), the architecture simplifies. The "Tokenize" step feeds into a "Linear layer" (pink with fire icon), which connects to the "Lightweight LM". The "Lightweight LM" now receives "Placeholder tokens" instead of explicit <cot> tokens. The output of the lightweight LM feeds into the LLM, which generates the final answer (Ans.). The "Implicit reasoning generator" is still present but operates implicitly, indicated by a dashed arrow pointing from the "Input query" to the lightweight LM. The "Custom. sent. trans." module is absent in this stage, suggesting it is frozen after training. The "Ground-truth reasoning" component is also absent, indicating it is only used during training. The "L_sem" metric is not shown, suggesting it is not used during inference. The "LLM" is shown as a single block, indicating it is the final output layer. The "Input query" is shown as a green box, indicating it is the input to the entire system. The "<cot>" tokens are shown as placeholder tokens, indicating they are not explicitly generated during inference but are implied by the lightweight LM. The "Lightweight LM" is shown as a single block, indicating it is the core component of the inference stage. The "LLM" is shown as a single block, indicating it is the final output layer. The "Ans." is shown as a single box, indicating it is the final output of the system."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents an overview of the RHO framework, structured into three main components labeled (a), (b), and (c), each enclosed in a dashed rectangular box, arranged horizontally from left to right. On the far left, a standalone \"Graph\" section shows a sample graph with gray circles representing unlabeled nodes and green circles representing labeled normal nodes; this serves as the input data. The central component (a), titled \"Adaptive Frequency-response Filter (AdaFreq)\", is divided into two sub-sections: \"Cross-channel representation (ccr)\" and \"Channel-wise representation (cwr)\", each with a corresponding graphical depiction and mathematical notation. In the ccr section, a grid of blue squares represents feature channels, which transform into a curve g(λ) plotted against λ, then map to a circular visualization showing nodes v_i and v_j with arrows indicating filter responses. Below it, the cwr section displays colored squares (red, yellow, cyan) representing different channels, again transforming into a multi-colored curve g(λ) and a circular visualization with arrows labeled \"Pull\" and \"Center\", accompanied by the loss term L_cwr. Both sections have arrows pointing to their respective visualizations. Component (b), \"Graph Normality Alignment (GNA)\", features a 3D-like visualization of nodes v_i and v_j projected onto a plane, with arrows labeled \"Inter-Push\" (red) and \"Intra-Push\" (blue) indicating alignment forces between nodes. A loss term L_GNA is shown below. Component (c), \"Anomaly Scoring\", displays a similar circular visualization with nodes v_i and v_j, a decision boundary line, and arrows indicating node positions relative to the center. The entire figure uses consistent color coding: red dots for high homophilic nodes, green dots for low homophilic nodes, and purple dots for abnormal nodes, as indicated in the legend. Arrows connect the outputs of (a) to (b) and (b) to (c), indicating a sequential workflow. The figure also includes a small inset in the top-right corner showing a legend for node types and homophily levels, with specific examples like H_i = 5/6 and H_j = 1/4. The overall layout follows a clear pipeline: input graph → AdaFreq (ccr & cwr) → GNA → Anomaly Scoring, with each stage contributing to the final anomaly detection."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the SILENCER framework, a multi-stage pipeline for generating a bias-neutralized benchmark ensemble through sample-wise and benchmark-wise debiasing. The entire process begins with a 'task description' block on the far left, which feeds into three parallel streams labeled 'Sample-Wise Debias', 'Question Domain Debias', 'Language Style Debias', and 'Wrong Label Debias'. These streams represent different types of bias mitigation strategies applied at the sample level. Each stream contains a sequence of modules connected by colored arrows indicating data flow between them. The first module in each stream is an 'Attribute Generation' block, followed by 'Attribute Integration', 'Benchmark Generation', 'Cross Paraphrase Generation', and finally 'Label Calibration'. Each module is represented by a colored box with internal layers showing different components or stages within the module. The colors correspond to different Large Language Models (LLMs) used in the process: green for one LLM, orange for another, yellow for a third, and blue for a fourth. The colored arrows show how each LLM processes the output from the previous stage and passes it to the next. After the final 'Label Calibration' stage, the outputs are aggregated into a 'Bias-Neutralizing Benchmark Ensemble'. This ensemble is represented as a grid-like structure labeled 'performance matrix', which shows the performance of the generated benchmarks across various metrics. The ensemble weights are calculated based on observed performance, which is fed back into the system via a loop labeled 'Iterate to convergence' to refine the ensemble weights until convergence is achieved. Finally, the refined ensemble weights are used to produce a 'debiased benchmark' denoted by D. The overall layout is organized horizontally, moving from left to right, with clear separation between the sample-wise debiasing stages and the final benchmark-wise debiasing stage. The diagram also includes a legend at the bottom indicating that the sample-wise debiasing consists of four components: Question Domain Debias, Language Style Debias, Wrong Label Debias, and an implicit fourth component implied by the structure. The visual design uses consistent shapes, colors, and arrow styles to convey the flow of information and the role of each component in the framework."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram presents an overview of a single-step inverse operator for conditioned time-series diffusion models, structured into two main processing blocks and a final scaling stage. On the left, inputs are defined as a noisy time series $\\mathbf{X}_{\\sigma(\\tau)}$ and a noise level $\\sigma(\\tau)$, both represented as purple circles, feeding into a green plus sign symbolizing their combination. This combined signal enters the top block labeled 'Frequency-Aware Block $B_\\theta(\\cdot, \\cdot)$'. Within this block, the input signal $\\mathbf{x}_{in}$ (shown as a green waveform) undergoes a forward Fourier transform $\\mathcal{F}$ (orange circle), producing $\\mathcal{F}(\\mathbf{x}_{in})$. This transformed signal is then processed by a teal rectangle labeled $\\Psi_\\theta(\\cdot)$, which applies a frequency-dependent filter. The output of $\\Psi_\\theta$ is inverted via $\\mathcal{F}^{-1}$ (another orange circle) to produce $\\mathbf{x}_{out}$, a denoised waveform. The entire block outputs $H_\\theta$, a scaled representation, shown as a teal rectangle labeled 'Scaling'. From $H_\\theta$, a downward arrow leads to a yellow rectangle labeled 'Flow Function' $\\phi(\\rho, \\tau, \\cdot)$, which represents the core denoising operator. This function outputs a final denoised signal $\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\sigma(\\rho)}$, depicted as a purple circle labeled 'Denoised'. Below the main flow, a second block titled 'Semigroup Consistency Training (Stage 2)' details the mechanism enabling the single-step operation. It begins with the same noisy input $\\mathbf{x}_{\\sigma(\\tau)}$ (purple circle), which passes through a yellow rectangle representing $\\phi(\\gamma, \\tau, \\cdot)$ to produce an intermediate denoised state $\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\sigma(\\gamma)}$ (purple circle). This intermediate state then passes through another yellow rectangle $\\phi(\\rho, \\gamma, \\cdot)$ to yield the final denoised output $\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\sigma(\\rho)}$. A third yellow rectangle, $\\phi(\\rho, \\tau, \\cdot)$, is shown below, with arrows connecting it to both the initial input and the final output, visually enforcing the semigroup consistency condition $\\phi(\\gamma, \\tau, \\cdot) \\circ \\phi(\\rho, \\gamma, \\cdot) = \\phi(\\rho, \\tau, \\cdot)$, which ensures the denoising process is consistent across different noise levels. The entire diagram is organized vertically, with the frequency-aware block at the top, the semigroup training block below it, and the final flow function and denoised output on the right side, illustrating a clear progression from noisy input to clean output through frequency-based filtering and semigroup-consistent transformation."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents a tripartite layout illustrating the process of generating a reference Student Response Graph (SRG) for educational assessment. On the left, labeled (a) 'Question Snippet', is a classroom-style prompt asking students to model particle behavior in water under different temperatures after placing a red-coated candy in each. It includes a photo of three dishes labeled 'Cold', 'Room Temperature', and 'Hot', alongside a blank model template and a video play button. Below this, section (b) 'Raw Textual Rubric' displays a proficiency matrix with three columns ('Proficient [2]', 'Developing [1]', 'Beginning [0]') evaluating how well students explain thermal energy's effect on particle motion, with specific criteria such as identifying water/dye particles, describing motion speed at different temperatures, and using correct terminology like 'faster/slower'. The rightmost panel, (c) 'Agent1’s Generated SRG (Gold-Standard)', is a directed graph centered around a pink node labeled 'Faster_Motion (Analyze)' connected to multiple blue nodes representing 'Understand' level concepts (e.g., 'Water_Particle_Hot', 'Dye_Particle_Cold'), and two green nodes labeled 'Apply' (e.g., 'Temperature_Increase', 'Temperature_Decrease'). A central yellow node labeled 'Key (Remember)' connects to 'Dye_Particle_Cold' and 'Slower_Motion (Analyze)', which links back to 'Water_Particle_Cold'. All nodes are circular, colored by cognitive level (blue=Understand, pink=Analyze, green=Apply, yellow=Remember), and arrows indicate hierarchical or conceptual relationships. The overall structure demonstrates how Agent 1 synthesizes the question, rubric, and gold-standard sketches into a Bloom’s taxonomy-ordered SRG, where nodes represent learning objectives and edges represent dependencies or progression, forming a scaffolded knowledge map for automated assessment."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the architecture of SWM-AP, a framework combining a Social World Model (SWM) and a Mechanism Design Policy for decision-making in complex social environments. The layout is horizontally divided into two main processing blocks: the left gray block labeled 'Social World Model' and the right blue block labeled 'Mechanism Design Policy', connected by bidirectional arrows indicating interaction. Above these blocks, an 'Environment buffer' (light beige cylinder) receives sampled trajectories from the 'Environment' (cityscape illustration), which also provides observations to the Mechanism Design Policy. A 'Design decision π_t' flows from the Mechanism Design Policy back to the Environment, influencing it via 'Tax; Location; Team' components. The Social World Model processes full trajectories to learn posterior trait representations, while the Mechanism Design Policy uses prior trait tracking for real-time decisions. Both modules engage in iterative policy learning using imagined trajectories generated through interaction. The diagram uses color-coded blocks (gray for SWM, blue for Mechanism Design Policy), arrows with labels (e.g., 'Policy Learning', 'Interaction'), and icons (e.g., globe, neural network, person at desk) to denote functional components and data flow."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates a three-stage computational pipeline for calculating the MonoSemanticity score (MS), labeled (A), (B), and (C), each representing a sequential step in processing a set of N input images. The overall layout is horizontal, flowing from left to right, with each stage clearly demarcated and connected via arrows indicating data flow.

In section (A), titled 'Extracting embeddings and activations', on the far left, there is a stack of N example images, visually represented as a vertical column of four distinct bird photos, with the bottommost image partially obscured. Below this stack, the notation {x₁, x₂, x₃, ..., x_N} indicates the input image set. Two parallel pathways branch out from this stack: one leads to a blue rectangular block labeled 'Vision Encoder E', which processes the images to produce embeddings. The other pathway leads to a vertically oriented, dashed-bordered module labeled 'SAE' (Sparse Autoencoder), which contains three vertically stacked colored circles—red at the top, yellow in the middle, and blue at the bottom—representing activation states. A small label φ^i appears above the SAE module, suggesting an index or parameter. An arrow points from the Vision Encoder E to a green-colored matrix below it, which contains N rows labeled E(x₁) through E(x_N), representing the extracted embeddings for each image. Another arrow points from the SAE module to the next stage.

Section (B), titled 'Pairwise similarity matrix', receives input from both the Vision Encoder E and the SAE module. From the Vision Encoder E, an arrow points to a large square matrix composed of N rows and N columns. Each row corresponds to an embedding E(x_i), and each cell within the matrix contains a pairwise similarity score, denoted s_ij (e.g., s₁₂, s₁₃, ..., s₁N, s₂₁, etc.). The matrix has a checkerboard pattern of shaded and unshaded cells, indicating computed similarity values. Simultaneously, from the SAE module, an arrow points to another similar-sized matrix, but this one contains activation values r_ij (e.g., r₁₂, r₁₃, ..., r₁N, r₂₁, etc.), also arranged in a checkerboard pattern. These matrices are visually aligned side-by-side, with the similarity matrix on the left and the activation matrix on the right. Both matrices are framed by a light gray border, and their respective labels are positioned directly beneath them.

Section (C), titled 'Activation-weighted average', takes inputs from both matrices in section (B). A curved arrow originates from the bottom of the similarity matrix and connects to a circular node labeled 'S'. Another curved arrow originates from the bottom of the activation matrix and connects to a circular node labeled 'R^k'. These two nodes converge into a single output node labeled 'MS^1', which represents the final MonoSemanticity score. Below this convergence point, a mathematical formula is displayed: MS^1 = [1/(N(N-1))] * Σ_{n,m≠n} r_nm * s_nm, where the summation symbol indicates summing over all pairs of neurons n and m (where n ≠ m), and the weights r_nm are multiplied by the corresponding similarity scores s_nm. The entire computation is enclosed within a light pink rectangular box labeled 'MS^1'. The figure uses consistent color coding: blue for encoders, green for embeddings, red/yellow/blue for SAE activations, and pink for the final output. All arrows are solid black lines with arrowheads pointing in the direction of data flow, ensuring clarity in the sequential nature of the process."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates the overall framework of a Sparse Diffusion Autoencoder, presented as a left-to-right flowchart depicting data transformation through encoding, diffusion, and denoising stages. The top row shows input data U_{1:t} as a sequence of stacked 2D heatmaps with red and blue gradients, transitioning via a vertical arrow labeled 'Re-encoding' to output U_{t:t+T}, which is visually identical but represents a future or denoised state. Below this, the Encoder ξ (a light blue box) takes U_{1:t} as input and produces discrete Representations z, shown as a stack of colored rectangular blocks (blue, red, orange), followed by Codewords c, which are similar blocks but with a more structured, grid-like arrangement. A horizontal arrow labeled 'Discrete' connects these two representations. The central component is a large light-blue shaded box titled 'Probe Topology', containing four interconnected graph structures labeled 'Spatiotemporal Dynamics'. Each graph has nodes connected by lines; some nodes are colored red, others blue, and one node per graph is highlighted in orange. An arrow labeled 'Adaption' points from the first graph to the second, indicating a sequential process over time t. A vertical arrow labeled 'Re-encoding' links the top row's U_{1:t} to the Probe Topology, suggesting feedback or iterative refinement. From the last graph in the Probe Topology, an arrow labeled 'Pad' leads to 'Starting points', depicted as a stack of partially filled heatmaps with red and blue regions. On the right side, a 'Denoise ε_θ' module (a light blue box) receives inputs from both the Probe Topology and a mathematical expression labeled 'Guide:', which includes terms like -1/√(1-α_t) * ε_θ - ζ ∇_{x_n} ||y - M(x_K)||². This guide term is connected to the Denoise module via a curved arrow. The Denoise module outputs a final result that feeds back into the U_{t:t+T} representation at the top right. The entire diagram uses consistent visual elements: heatmaps for continuous data, stacked blocks for discrete representations, and graphs for modeling dynamics. Mathematical formulas are embedded within the diagram to define operations such as h_t = h_0 + ∫₀ᵗ (A-I)*h_s ds, h_0 = φ(v), v_i = ψ(h_i), and a(h_i, h_j) = softmax((W_K h_i)^T W_Q h_j + W_E e_ij / d_k). These equations are positioned near relevant components to indicate their functional role in the architecture."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates a comprehensive deep learning framework for person detection and re-identification, organized into two main horizontal rows. The top row details the spatiotemporal consensus mechanism across source and target domains, while the bottom row outlines the core processing modules: backbone, detection, re-ID, task cooperation, and scene prior contrastive learning. The entire architecture is designed to enhance performance through cross-task collaboration and scene-aware consistency.

In the top row, on the far left, two stacks labeled 'Source' and 'Target' represent input image sequences. These feed into a 'Memory Bank' which stores 'Last Labels', 'Scores', 'BBoxes', and 'Embeddings'. From this memory bank, 'Temporal Consensus' processes data via a feedback loop. The output feeds into two parallel consensus pipelines: 'Spatiotemporal Consensus for Det.' and 'Spatiotemporal Consensus for Re-ID'. The 'Detection' branch uses 'IBEM' (Image-Based Embedding Measurement) to compute 'Measure Distances' between frames, then applies 'Spatial Consensus' and 'Temporal Consensus' to refine embeddings. The 'Re-ID' branch similarly applies 'Spatial Consensus' and 'Temporal Consensus' to align representations over time and space.

The bottom row begins with a 'Backbone' (Conv 1-4) that extracts features from input images. These features are passed to a 'Detection Head' which outputs detection results. The detection head branches into three streams: 'Detection Level DD' (Domain Discrimination), 'Re-ID Level DD', and 'Image Level DD', each applying GRL (Gradient Reversal Layer) to enforce domain-invariant representations. The detection head also connects to an 'Attention Embedder' module, which takes feature maps (24x12Map and 12x6Map) from Conv5, concatenates them, and upsamples to produce pixel-level embeddings. These embeddings undergo 'Mean of Pixels' computation before being fed into the 'Scene Prior CL' module.

The 'Task Cooperation' module, positioned centrally, receives inputs from both detection and re-ID streams. It includes a 'Person' stack and a 'Background' stack, with arrows indicating 'Increase' and 'Decrease' respectively, suggesting dynamic weighting based on foreground/background presence. The 'Scene Prior CL' module processes these embeddings using 'Calculate Proxies', which involves EMA (Exponential Moving Average), FIFO (First-In-First-Out), PT (Projection Transform), LUT (Look-Up Table), and CQ (Contrast Quantization). These components work together to generate proxies for contrastive learning. The final output from Scene Prior CL is used to compute 'Cross Entropy' loss, which jointly optimizes detection and re-ID tasks. The entire architecture emphasizes multi-level consistency, cross-domain alignment, and scene-aware representation learning."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates SpecEM, a training-free plug-and-play LLM ensemble framework, structured into three main stages: Drafting Stage (blue), Verification Stage (light orange), and Online Feedback (light green), arranged vertically from top to bottom. Each stage is enclosed in a distinct colored rectangular box with rounded corners and labeled accordingly. The entire process is iterative, indicated by dashed arrows looping back from the final output to earlier stages.

In the Drafting Stage, three candidate outputs C1(k), C2(k), and C3(k) are generated from three different models (Model 1, Model 2, Model 3), each represented by a unique icon (a robot face, an 'M' logo, and a 'G' logo respectively). These candidates are derived from a prior context π^(k-1), which is shown as a text box below the models asking "How does one work a computer mouse? ...". The three candidates are visually listed with example text: C1(k) = "A computer mouse is a simple input device", C2(k) = "Here's a step-by-step guide", and C3(k) = "Working a computer mouse is a skill". A "Concat" operation combines these candidates into a single vector, denoted as z^(k-1), which feeds into the Verification Stage via a solid blue arrow.

The Verification Stage takes the concatenated input and applies three models (again represented by the same icons) to evaluate each candidate. Each model produces a score matrix for each candidate, such as Model 1 producing scores s1,1^(k), s1,2^(k), s1,3^(k) for C1(k), C2(k), C3(k) respectively. These matrices are then multiplied by corresponding weight vectors ω1^(k), ω2^(k), ω3^(k), and summed to produce a final score for each candidate. The final scores y1^(k), y2^(k), y3^(k) are displayed in a row, and the highest-scoring candidate is selected using "Select Top 1 Candidate". This selection result is shown in the rightmost box under the Verification Stage, where C2(k) is marked with a green checkmark and C1(k), C3(k) are marked with red crosses, indicating it was chosen as the best output.

The Online Feedback stage, located below the Verification Stage, receives the selected candidate and calculates a reward based on its quality. The reward calculation is shown as a formula: "Calculate Reward" followed by "ωi^(k-1) · e^ηy_i^k → ωi^(k)", indicating that the weights are updated using the reward signal. This updated weight vector is then fed back into the Verification Stage via a dashed arrow, suggesting that the weights are refreshed for the next iteration. Additionally, the selected candidate C2^(k) is passed to a "Single-Round-Output" box at the bottom right, which contains the final output: "Here's a step-by-step guide". A dashed arrow loops from this output back to the Drafting Stage, indicating that the best candidate from the current round becomes the new prior context for the next iteration. The entire workflow is designed to iteratively refine the output through feedback-driven weight updates without requiring retraining."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents four interconnected diagrams illustrating a multi-stage deep learning architecture for hyperspectral imaging, labeled (a), (b), (c), and (d). Diagram (a) shows the overall architecture of CIDNet with K iterative stages. It begins with initialization from three input images (y, H) which feed into a sequence of K identical processing blocks. Each block contains an element-wise multiplication (denoted by a circle with '×') between an input tensor y and a weight matrix W(k), followed by a projection function proj(·) and a proximal operator prox_R(·). The output z(k) is fed back into the next stage. The final output is a 3D tensor G representing the reconstructed hyperspectral cube. A legend on the right defines symbols: concatenation (C), element-wise multiplication (circle with ×), matrix multiplication (×), weighted-sum (Σ), down-sampling (Down), up-sampling (Up), LayerNorm (LN), and 1x1 convolution (1x1). Diagram (b) illustrates the CASSI system workflow: starting with a chromaticity cube, it applies a mask to produce a modulated HSI, which is then dispersed into multiple channels before being imaged and measured as Y. Diagram (c) details the asymmetric hybrid spatial-spectral transformer (HSST) backbone. It features an encoder-decoder structure where the encoder uses Spa-LWSA (local window spatial attention) modules and downsampling layers, while the decoder employs Spec-TKSA (sparse top-K spectral self-attention) modules and upsampling layers. Fusion operations combine outputs from different levels, and the architecture maintains channel dimensions through convolutional layers and normalization. Diagram (d) provides a close-up of the Spec-TKSA module. It takes an input tensor X and splits it into query Q, key K, and value V tensors via 1x1 convolutions. These are used to compute attention weights through a scaled dot-product mechanism (Q·K^T), which is then passed through a softmax function to produce a mask. This mask is applied to the value tensor V, and the result is aggregated using a weighted sum (Σ) to produce the final output. All diagrams are color-coded and use consistent notation to represent tensor dimensions and operations."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates the SplitFlow architecture, divided into two main sections: Decomposition (Sec. 4.1) and Composition (Sec. 4.2). On the left side, a source image of a German Shepherd dog (x₀ˢʳᶜ) is decomposed into multiple feature representations (xₜ¹, xₜ², xₜ³) using a latent noise variable ε ~ N(0,1). These decomposed features are processed through separate, color-coded sub-flows: purple for xₜ¹, blue for xₜ², and orange for xₜ³, each leading to target feature representations x₀¹, x₀², x₀³ respectively. A red box labeled 't_proj' indicates a projection step applied to the intermediate feature xₜ¹. The right side shows the composition phase where these sub-targets are aggregated into a unified final target feature x₀ᴱ. This process is guided by an LLM (indicated by a large downward arrow), which receives a source caption ('A german shepherd dog stands on the grass with mouth [closed]') and a target caption ('A german shepherd dog [with black sunglasses] [jumping] on the grass with mouth [opened]'). The LLM generates semantic captions (φᵗᵍᵗ¹, φᵗᵍᵗ², φᵗᵍᵗ³) that split the target caption into components corresponding to each sub-flow. Below, four images visually represent the output of each sub-flow: the first image (purple border) shows a dog wearing black sunglasses, the second (blue border) shows a dog jumping with open mouth, the third (orange border) shows a dog with black sunglasses, and the fourth (no border) shows the final composed image combining both attributes. The entire diagram uses arrows to indicate data flow from source to decomposition, then to composition, and finally to the generated outputs."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents the sPHNN model architecture in two parts: (a) a computation graph detailing the forward pass, and (b) a visualization of the resulting dynamics. In part (a), the layout is divided into three vertical sections: 'Input', 'Operations', and 'Output'. The 'Input' section on the left contains two light blue rounded rectangles labeled 'x' and 'u', representing state and control inputs. These inputs feed into the 'Operations' section, which is further subdivided into 'Parameters' and 'FFNN + reshape' modules. The 'Parameters' module contains a pink rounded rectangle labeled 'x*', representing learned parameters, connected via an arrow to a larger pink rounded rectangle labeled 'FICNN' (Finite Element-based Convolutional Neural Network). The FICNN has a visual structure of interconnected nodes (blue circles) with red nodes at the edges, indicating a neural network architecture. An arrow from 'x*' to 'FICNN' indicates parameterization. The output of the FICNN, denoted by 'f', feeds into a yellow rounded rectangle labeled 'H = f(x) + f_norm(x, x*)', representing the total Hamiltonian. Below this, another yellow rectangle shows the dynamical equation: 'dot{x} = (J - R) ∂H/∂x + Gu', where 'J' and 'R' are derived from separate FFNNs. Three stacked pink rounded rectangles labeled 'FFNN + reshape' each represent one of these components: 'J', 'L', and 'G'. Each FFNN has a similar node structure to the FICNN. The 'J' FFNN output is labeled 'J', the 'L' FFNN output is labeled 'L', and the 'G' FFNN output is labeled 'G'. The 'J' output connects to the equation via an upward arrow, while 'L' connects to a yellow rectangle labeled 'R = LL^T', indicating the symmetric matrix R is computed from L. The 'G' output connects directly to the dynamical equation. All three FFNN outputs feed into the final equation, which then outputs 'dot{x}' in a light blue rounded rectangle on the right under 'Output'. In part (b), titled 'Composition of the total dynamics', there are two plots side-by-side. The left plot, labeled 'Conservative', shows a vector field with concentric circular arrows around the origin, indicating rotational motion. The right plot, labeled 'Dissipative', shows a vector field with radial arrows pointing inward toward the origin, indicating energy dissipation. A large arrow points from the 'Conservative' plot to the 'Dissipative' plot, indicating their combination forms the 'Total dynamics' plot shown above them, which displays a hybrid vector field combining both rotational and radial components. The entire figure illustrates how the sPHNN decomposes complex dynamics into interpretable conservative and dissipative components."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents the overall framework of StreamFlow, divided into three main sections labeled (a), (b), and (c), each describing a different component or stage of the model. Section (a), titled 'Architecture of StreamFlow', shows the core processing pipeline. It begins with a 24 kHz waveform input, which is fed into an 'Audio Codec Encoder' (depicted as a light blue box with a star icon). The output from this encoder is split into two streams: one goes to a 'Token Embedder' (light blue box) and the other to a 'Linear → Reshape' block (white boxes) that processes noise. Both streams converge into an 'Input Embedder' (light purple rounded rectangle). This embedder feeds into a central 'Scale-DiT Block' (yellow rounded rectangle), which is the main transformer-based module. Above it, a 'Final Block' (light purple) connects to a 'Streaming Flow Matching loss' module (red text above a light purple box containing 'Reshape' and 'Linear'). A 'Noise x_t' stream (black gradient bar) with a '[MASK]' token (purple) is also fed into the Input Embedder via a 'Masked Prompt' path. Additional inputs include 'PosEmb' (position embedding) and 'Time step' (white box), both feeding into the Scale-DiT Block. Section (b), titled 'Prompt with Causal Noising', illustrates the temporal handling of audio data. It features a top waveform visualization split into 'Context Prompt' (purple) and 'Streaming window' (gray). Below, a timeline labeled 'CFM Timestep t' ranges from 1.0 to 0.0, corresponding to a 'Waveform frame index' sequence from 0 to L. Two tables show 'Data stream' components: 'x_1^(k)...x_k^(k)' for past context and 'x_{t+1}^(k)...x_L^(k)' for streaming sequence, with time steps t=1 and t=1-λ, λ∈[0,1]. Section (c), titled 'Fine-tuning with Adversarial Training', describes the downstream fine-tuning phase. It starts with a 24 kHz waveform input again, processed by an 'Audio Codec Encoder' (light blue box with star). The output feeds into a 'Pre-trained StreamFlow' module (yellow rounded rectangle with a flame icon). This module has two loss branches: 'Reconstruction loss' (pink box labeled 'Multi-scale Mel-spec') and 'Adversarial loss' (red text). The adversarial loss branches into three discriminators: 'MPD' (green trapezoid), 'MS-SB-CQTD' (green trapezoid), and 'MS-STFTD' (green trapezoid). All three discriminators receive input from the Pre-trained StreamFlow module. The entire figure uses consistent color coding: yellow for core modules, light purple for embedding layers, light blue for encoders, pink for reconstruction loss, green for adversarial discriminators, and red for loss labels. Arrows indicate data flow direction, with solid lines for primary connections and dashed lines for auxiliary or conceptual links."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates the SENATOR framework, a multi-step iterative process for knowledge graph exploration and repair using Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) and an LLM agent. The global layout is divided into two main vertical sections: the left side shows the MCTS-based search process with a tree structure, and the right side details the iterative loop of selection, expansion, simulation, and backup steps. A horizontal flowchart at the top outlines the core four-phase cycle: (a) Selection → (b) Expansion → (c) Simulation → (d) Backup, which is repeated X times. Below this loop, a separate trajectory path labeled (f) Max SE Trajectory shows the sequence of states S₀ to Sₙ connected by transition probabilities p₀ to pₙ, indicating the optimal path found through the search. The bottom section includes modules for data generation (g) and knowledge repair (h), which feed back into the system. The visual modules include: a root node (gray circle), current node (green circle), node (white circle), expanded node (light green circle), terminal node (green triangle), synthetic data (stacked disks), LLM agent (robot icon), and KG (knowledge graph, represented by a network of nodes and edges). The tree structure on the left visually represents the MCTS exploration, starting from a root node and branching out through levels S₀, S₁, S₂, ..., Sₙ, each level showing nodes connected by dashed lines representing relations. At each step, arrows indicate transitions: from selection (a) to expansion (b), where a seed entity is chosen; from expansion (b) to simulation (c), where the LLM agent generates a trajectory; and from simulation (c) to backup (d), where value signals V(sₜ) are backpropagated via a red wavy arrow pointing downward to a terminal node. The MCTS module is shown interacting with the LLM agent via bidirectional arrows, indicating feedback loops for exploration and refinement. The 'Max SE Exploration' label points to the MCTS tree, emphasizing its role in maximizing structural entropy. The 'Prompt' box feeds into the data generation module (g), which uses synthetic data to generate new knowledge. The 'Knowledge Repair' module (h) receives input from the synthetic data and outputs back to the MCTS tree, completing the cycle. The entire process is driven by the iterative loop, with the goal of finding the best trajectory (f) through the knowledge graph, which is then used to generate data and repair knowledge gaps. The figure also includes a legend at the top explaining the meaning of each symbol, such as root node, current node, terminal node, etc., ensuring clarity in interpreting the diagram."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents three distinct methodologies for generating differentially private synthetic data, arranged horizontally across three colored sections. The top-left section, labeled 'DP Finetuning' in a light blue box, shows a linear pipeline: a 'Pretrained Diffusion Model' receives 'Private Data (Images)' and undergoes 'DP fine-tune' to produce a 'Finetuned Diffusion Model', which then generates 'DP-Synthetic Data'. The top-right section, labeled 'Private Evolution: Images' in a light green box, depicts an iterative loop: a 'Diffusion API' generates initial 'Samples', which are compared with 'Private Data (Selected Samples)'. Based on this comparison, 'Generate New Samples' are produced, feeding back into the loop to refine the output, ultimately yielding 'DP-Synthetic Data'. The bottom section, labeled 'Synthesis via Private Textual Intermediaries (Ours)' in a light yellow box, outlines a two-stage process: 'Private Data (Images)' serves as a reference. It feeds into 'Random Generated Text Data', which passes through 'Through Aug-PE' (with a 'Pre-trained language model' icon) to become 'DP-Synthetic Text Data'. This text data is then processed by a 'Diffusion Model' to generate the final output, 'DP-Synthetic Data'. All components are represented by icons (e.g., neural network for models, document for text, stack of images for data), connected by arrows indicating data flow. The entire diagram visually contrasts direct model fine-tuning, iterative image evolution, and a novel text-intermediate synthesis approach."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the TAMO framework for tabular LLMs, showing a dual-path processing pipeline that combines structured table representation with textual serialization before feeding into large language models. On the left, a table is processed through a 'Unified Hypergraph Modeling' module, depicted as stacked blue and pink horizontal bars, which feeds into a 'Hypergraph-enhanced Tabular Encoder' (orange rounded rectangle with flame icon). This encoder outputs 'Table Structure Emb.' (three yellow blocks inside a dashed box), which then passes through an 'Alignment Projector' (orange rounded rectangle with flame icon) before being fed into the LLMs. On the right, the same table is serialized into a 'Formatted Text Sequence' (text block with '[TAB] ... | col : ... | row1: ...') via a 'Serialize Table into' label, which generates 'Textual Tokens Embeddings' (three green blocks inside a dashed box). Both the table structure embeddings and textual tokens embeddings feed into 'LLMs (Llama / Mistral / ...)' (blue rounded rectangle with snowflake icon), which then produces an 'Answer' (dashed rectangle). An optional 'LoRA' module (orange rounded rectangle with flame icon) is shown connected to the LLMs. A small example table at the bottom shows columns 'Year', 'Title', 'Role', 'Notes' with sample rows. Arrows indicate data flow from inputs to outputs, with dashed arrows indicating optional or intermediate steps."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the Tensor Product Attention (TPA) mechanism within the Tensor Product Attention Transformer (T6), showing a multi-head attention architecture. At the top, a 'Linear' layer receives input and feeds into a 'Concat' layer, which combines outputs from multiple attention heads before passing to another 'Linear' layer for final projection. Below this, a large purple block labeled 'Scaled Dot-Product Attention' represents the core attention computation, with a subscript 'h' indicating multiple heads. This block receives three parallel streams corresponding to Query (Q), Key (K), and Value (V) components. Each stream begins with a 'Scale' module (blue box) that applies scaling factors 1/Rq, 1/Rk, or 1/Rv respectively. These scaled outputs feed into a pink circular multiplication node, which performs element-wise multiplication between two tensors. For the Q and K streams, the inputs to the multiplication node are A (green triangle labeled 'Linear') and B (green triangle labeled 'Linear'), where B undergoes an additional 'RoPE' (Rotary Position Embedding) transformation (orange rectangle) before being fed into the multiplication. The V stream follows the same structure but uses AV and BV. All three streams converge into the Scaled Dot-Product Attention block. The entire structure is symmetric across the three streams, indicating identical processing for Q, K, and V. The diagram uses consistent visual cues: green triangles for Linear layers, blue boxes for Scale operations, pink circles for multiplication, and orange rectangles for RoPE. Arrows indicate data flow from bottom to top through the attention mechanism, with feedback loops from the multiplication nodes back to the Linear layers, suggesting residual connections or iterative refinement. The layout is hierarchical, with the attention block at the center receiving inputs from three parallel branches, emphasizing the tensor product nature of the attention computation."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure is divided into three main vertical sections labeled 'Critique Generation', 'Overview of CGI', and 'Action Refinement', each with distinct color-coded headers (blue, light blue, and blue respectively). The leftmost section, 'Critique Generation', targets 'Weak Feedback' and shows an initial 'Action': 'Go to bathroom'. A 'Critic' module evaluates this, generating a critique: 'Go to bathroom highly contributes to current task.' Below, it details 'Critique Structure' with dimensions like Construction, Feasibility, Efficiency, a Grade scale from Excellent to Very Poor, and Suggested Revision fields. This section includes two steps: Step 1 'Distilled Expert Critique' showing a sequence of states and actions (x₀, a₀, s₀, ..., xₜ, aₜ, sₜ), and Step 2 'Training Critic model by distilled critique' using dataset D_critic = {(τₜ, aₜ, cₜ)}. A downward arrow points from this section to the central 'Overview of CGI' section. The central section, 'Overview of CGI', presents a task: 'measure the temperature of unknown substance B located around the bathroom'. It shows an 'Actor' buffer A_t containing four actions: A1 (Pick up thermometer), A2 (Focus on Substance), A3 (Go to kitchen), A4 (Go to bathroom). The 'Critic' evaluates action A4, providing a critique: 'Contribution: Agent skips the crucial step of picking up the thermometer...' and a 'Suggested Revision': 'The agent should Pick up thermometer before attempting to go to bathroom.' The 'Actor' then generates a 'Refined Action aₜ': 'Pick up thermometer' based on this critique. The rightmost section, 'Action Refinement', targets 'Poor Utilization'. It shows a 'Thought' process: 'I have to first go to bathroom to get close to substance B.' It includes a 'Collect Training Data by πᵏ' module with inputs D_refine (History) and D_correct (Task Description), outputs Refined Action, and processes 'Critique Utilization' and 'Enhance Reasoning'. Below, the 'Update Actor Policy' module uses filtered datasets D_refine and D_correct to improve the policy via R(τ). Arrows connect the sections: from Critique Generation to Overview of CGI, and from Overview of CGI to Action Refinement, indicating a sequential workflow. The entire diagram illustrates a loop where the actor generates actions, the critic provides feedback, and the actor refines actions based on critique, with mechanisms to improve both critic and actor models over time."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram presents a flowchart of a method called Role-Aware Reasoning (RAR), designed to generate high-fidelity, in-character responses from large language models by mitigating attention diversion and style drift. The layout is vertically structured with a top-down workflow, starting with user input and ending with the final response, with lateral branches for auxiliary modules. At the top left, a dashed box labeled 'User Input X' contains a cartoon avatar of Peter Parker and his question: 'Peter Parker, how do you balance your personal life and superhero responsibilities?' This flows via a red arrow into a central white rectangular module labeled 'LARGE REASON MODEL'. From this, a gray downward arrow leads to a second white rectangular module labeled 'Role-Aware Reasoning Distillation', which contains a green-bordered speech bubble with the text 'I’m Spider-Man!!!' and a small Spider-Man mask icon. A green arrow from the left side points to this distillation module, originating from a box labeled 'Role Identity Activation (RIA) C_R', which contains four smaller boxes: 'Emotions', 'Experiences', 'Standpoints', and 'Motivations', each with an icon. Below the distillation module, a gray downward arrow connects to a white rectangular module labeled 'LARGE LANGUAGE MODEL'. From this, a green arrow points upward to a box labeled 'Character Profile(Peter Parker)', which details his personality ('Quick witted, intelligent...'), growth ('Shy teen, confident hero'), catchphrase ('With great power comes...'), and speech style ('Informal, witty, self-deprecating...'). Another green arrow from the distillation module points rightward to a box labeled 'Reasoning Style Optimization (RSO)', which contains two variables: 'χ_Logic' and 'χ_Story', and a 2x2 grid with labels 'Choose' and 'Reject' for both axes, forming four cells: 'Choose/Choose', 'Choose/Reject', 'Reject/Choose', and 'Reject/Reject'. The grid is annotated with 'C_Fact' and 'C_Know' indicating conditions. A blue curly brace groups three sub-boxes below the grid labeled 'Style Core', 'Focus', 'Features', and 'Context'. From the RSO module, a green arrow points down to a white rectangular box labeled 'Role-Aware Thought y_COT', containing a thought process: 'I am... The person just said... First, I feel... Second, based on my experience/knowledge... Then, I need to consider... So, I'm planning to...' This flows via a red arrow to a final output box labeled 'High-Fidelity, In-Character Response y_ans', which contains a sample response: 'Balancing? It’s like trying to juggle homework, a lab report, and a crook fleeing the scene all at once. (sighs, runs a hand through hair)... Use my spider-sense to, uh, *estimate* how bad a situation is before dropping everything... Now, if you’ll excuse me, the bell’s about to ring, and I heard something suspicious near the science lab... (dashes off, leaving a faint trail of webbing)'. On the top right, a dashed box labeled 'Rigid Thought y_COT' contains a list of four steps: '1. Acknowledge query. 2. State event. 3. Provide factual details. 4. Conclude.' Below it, a brain icon is connected by a line to a text box stating 'It’s an imperfect system. There have been instances of missed... The core idea is to maintain a connection to one’s humanity...', followed by 'Inconsistent Response y_ans'. The entire diagram uses a clean white background with black text, colored arrows (red for primary flow, green for auxiliary connections), and icons to represent concepts. The caption explains that the method addresses the limitations of traditional reasoning by incorporating RIA to activate role traits and RSO to guide reasoning style dynamically."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the iterative workflow of ACTMED, an LLM-based clinical decision support system, structured into three main vertical sections: 'Clinical environment', 'LLM-problem solving agent', and 'Natural language output space'. Each section contains interconnected components represented by colored boxes, arrows, and numbered steps indicating the sequence of operations.

In the 'Clinical environment' section (left), a cartoon clinician icon is positioned below a green speech bubble labeled 'Problem statement S with knowledge K_t', representing the initial query. An arrow labeled '1' points from this box to the agent's first step. Below, another green box labeled 'Updated problem statement K_{t+1} \leftarrow K_t \cup \hat{u}_t^{(i*)}' indicates the updated knowledge state after a test is performed. This box connects via arrow '6' to a green box at the bottom labeled 'Obtain true test result \hat{u}_t^{(i*)}', which then feeds back via arrow '5' to the 'Natural language output space' section. Arrow '7' loops from the updated knowledge box back to the initial problem statement, completing the feedback loop.

The central 'LLM-problem solving agent' section (middle) is enclosed in a light blue dashed rectangle. It begins with a yellow box labeled 'Sample from prior p_{prior}^{(j)} \sim P(y_{d_t}=1 | K_t)', representing the agent's initial probability estimation based on existing knowledge. Arrow '2' leads from this box to the next yellow box labeled 'Sample test results u_t^{(i,j)} \sim P(u_t^{(i)})', indicating simulated test outcomes. Arrow '3' connects this to the third yellow box labeled 'Sample from posterior p_{post}^{(j)} \sim P(y_{d_t}=1 | K_t, u_t^{(i,j)})', showing the agent's belief update after incorporating simulated test data. Arrow '4' directs from the posterior box to the rightmost section. All three yellow boxes are vertically aligned and have rounded corners.

The 'Natural language output space' section (right) is enclosed in a light gray dashed rectangle. It contains three green rectangular boxes arranged vertically. The top box, labeled '① Prior distribution', contains the formula p_{prior}^{(j)} for j=1 to M. The middle box, labeled '② Test results', contains u_t^{(i,j)} for each test i. The bottom box, labeled '③ Posterior distribution', contains p_{post}^{(i,j)} for each test i. These three boxes are connected by arrows forming a cycle: arrow '4' enters from the agent's posterior box, arrow '4' exits to a box labeled 'Select test with highest utility', which contains the formula i^* = arg max_i \mathcal{F}(\mathbb{E}[KL(\mathbb{B}(p_{post}) || \mathbb{B}(p_{prior})) / c(u_t^{(i)})]. This selection box has two arrows: one looping back to the 'Posterior distribution' box (implying re-evaluation or iteration within the selection phase) and another leading to the 'Obtain true test result' box in the clinical environment. The entire structure forms a closed-loop iterative process where the agent continuously refines its predictions and selects the most informative test based on expected information gain."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the TransGesture framework, a transformer-based architecture for gesture target estimation guided by human gaze. The global layout is divided into three main vertical processing streams: head-related inputs on the left, body-related inputs at the bottom, and a central fusion module connecting them. These streams converge into a joint transformer module, which feeds into two parallel output heads for mask and existence prediction. The structure follows a clear data flow from input encoding through transformer decoding to final prediction.

On the left, the 'Head Mask of Subject Person' (black box with pink rectangle) connects via a '+' node to a 'Head Patch Encoding' block (pink box), whose output combines with the gaze feature Z_gaze (blue vector) before entering the 'Gaze Decoder' (purple box). This decoder contains multiple 'Transformer Block' units (yellow rectangles) and produces F_gaze (purple vector). Similarly, at the bottom, the 'Body Mask of Subject Person' (black box with green rectangle) connects to a 'Body Patch Encoding' block (green box), whose output combines with Z_gesture (blue vector) before feeding into the 'Gesture Decoder' (green box), which also consists of multiple 'Transformer Block' units (yellow rectangles) and produces F_gesture (green vector).

A central 'Joint Cross Attention Transformer' module (brown box) receives both F_gaze and F_gesture as inputs. It has three distinct input branches labeled K, V, and Q (represented by purple, green, and light green vectors respectively), each connected to a 'Cross Attention Transformer Block'. The module is visually marked with a flame icon indicating heat or activation. The output of this joint module is split into two paths: one leading to the 'Mask Head' (gray box with flame icon) producing Y_mask (black box with yellow silhouette), and another leading to the 'Exist Head' (light gray box with flame icon) producing Y_exist (black box with white text). Both output heads are shown with upsampled features (indicated by small squares) before final prediction.

The initial visual input is a real-world image (bottom-left) showing people interacting, with bounding boxes highlighting subjects. This image feeds into a 'DINOv2' encoder (blue trapezoid) which extracts scene tokens Z (blue vector). Z is then passed through two 'Linear' layers (black boxes) to produce Z_gaze and Z_gesture, which are added to the respective head/body patch encodings. The diagram uses color coding: purple for gaze-related components, green for gesture-related components, and brown for the joint module. All transformer blocks are rectangular with rounded corners, and arrows indicate directional data flow. Equations such as Z_gaze and Z_gesture are annotated near their respective connections. The overall workflow demonstrates how gaze and gesture information are encoded separately, fused via cross-attention, and used to predict both presence and spatial location of gestures."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates a reinforcement learning fine-tuning framework for multimodal generation. At the bottom left, an input prompt \"How to saute potatoes? Predict the subsequent step. The previous steps are: Wash the potatoes.\" is shown alongside a visual image of hands washing potatoes. This input feeds into two encoders: a green \"Text encoder\" processing text tokens and a yellow \"Vision encoder\" processing discrete visual tokens. These encoded representations converge into a light blue \"Policy model\" marked with a fire icon, indicating it is a trained model. The Policy model generates two sequences of completion tokens — \"Completion tokens 1\" (light green squares) and \"Completion tokens 2\" (light green squares) — which are then decoded into human-readable \"Completions\". Completion 1 shows \"<think>Step1: Wash your potatoes under the cold water</think> <vis> [image of hand washing potatoes]</vis>\" and Completion 2 shows \"<think>Step1: Cut the potatoes.</think> <vis> [image of knife cutting potatoes]</vis>\". These completions are fed into a \"Reward models\" box marked with a snowflake icon, indicating it is a frozen model. The reward models output \"Hybrid rewards\" as numerical scores like \"0.75/0.54/1.00\" and \"0.95/0.76/0.00\", representing scores for Textual, Visual, and Format dimensions respectively. These hybrid rewards are then transformed into \"Group relative advantages\" (e.g., 1 and -1). Simultaneously, the completion tokens from the Policy model are passed to a \"Reference model\" (also marked with a snowflake icon), which outputs \"Token-level advantages\" visualized as three overlapping probability density curves (green solid, green dashed, yellow dashed). A KL divergence term labeled \"KL\" connects the Reference model's output back to the Policy model, indicating KL regularization. A legend at the bottom clarifies that fire icons denote \"Trained models\" and snowflake icons denote \"Frozen models\". The entire process forms a feedback loop where policy optimization is guided by both the group relative advantages from the reward models and the KL divergence term."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents a flowchart of the TrackingWorld method, which transforms 2D pixel-level tracking into world-centric 3D object trajectories. The layout is divided into three main vertical sections: Preprocessing (left), Lifting 2D Tracks to World-centric 3D Tracks (center), and Dynamic Object Tracking (right), connected by directional arrows indicating data flow. The left section begins with input video frames, processed through three parallel modules: Tracker (outputting Sparse Tracks), Depth Estimator (outputting Depth Maps), and Mask Estimator (outputting Dynamic Masks). These outputs feed into a 'Dense 2D Tracking for Every Pixel' module, which uses DELTA to compute Tracking Point Features, an Upsampler, and a 'Tracking Every Frame' block to produce Dense Tracks. The center section, titled 'Lifting 2D Tracks to World-centric 3D Tracks', takes these dense tracks as input. It performs 'Initial Pose Estimation' using intra-clip pose estimation across consecutive clips (Clip t and Clip t+1). This is followed by 'Inter-Clip Pose Estimation'. The output of this stage feeds into 'Dynamic Background Refinement', where two 3D car models are shown with transformation matrices T_static and T'_static, illustrating how camera poses are refined to separate static from dynamic backgrounds. A downward arrow leads to the final section, 'Dynamic Object Tracking', which shows multiple video frames with 3D bounding boxes around vehicles, labeled 'Lifting', indicating the final step of reconstructing 3D trajectories of dynamic objects based on the refined camera poses. All components are represented as rectangular blocks with descriptive labels, some colored (e.g., pink for tracking modules, gray for pose estimation), and arrows indicate the direction of data or control flow. The entire process is designed to handle newly emerging objects and convert 2D pixel tracks into accurate 3D spatial trajectories."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the REAL framework, a reinforcement learning-based approach for generating secure and functional code. At the top center, a 'Policy Gradient' module receives feedback from two evaluators and updates the policy model. On the left, a 'Task Description' box defines the problem: counting items in a database within a user-specified price range. This task description feeds into a 'Policy Model', represented by a neural network icon with multiple nodes, which generates a 'Generated Program'—a Python script using sqlite3 to execute a SQL query with user inputs. The generated program is then evaluated by two parallel modules: the 'Vulnerability Detector' (via Program Analysis) and the 'Functionality Verifier' (via Unit Tests). The Vulnerability Detector converts the program into a Control Flow Graph (CFG), performs Taint Analysis to detect SQL injection vulnerabilities, and outputs a binary reward ('Pass or Fail'). The Functionality Verifier runs unit tests (e.g., Test 1: $2.5-$10→3, Test 2: $3-$1→0) and calculates a reward based on the ratio of passed tests to total tests. These two rewards are combined into a 'Hybrid Reward' signal, which is fed back to the Policy Gradient module to refine the policy model. The entire process forms a closed-loop reinforcement learning system aimed at producing code that is both functionally correct and free of security vulnerabilities."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents the architecture of TrajMamba, divided into two main components labeled (a) Travel Purpose-aware Pre-training and (b) Knowledge Distillation Pre-training, arranged side-by-side horizontally. In section (a), on the left, POIs (Points of Interest) and Trajectories are input into separate encoders: the POI Textual Encoder and Road Textual Encoder. Each encoder processes positive (Pos+) and negative (Neg-) views, which are connected via bidirectional arrows labeled 'Pull together' and 'Push apart' respectively, feeding into a shared 'Full-Trajectory Embedding' module at the bottom. The POI Textual Encoder is depicted as a gray rounded rectangle, while the Road Textual Encoder is similarly styled but positioned to the right. Above these encoders, a map icon with a house and car symbolizes POIs, and a series of red diamond-shaped trajectory markers labeled T1 through T6 are shown above the road network, which is represented by a black node-and-edge graph. These trajectories are matched to the road network, and then passed to the 'Traj-Mamba Encoder', a red-bordered box, which outputs to the Full-Trajectory Embedding. Section (b), on the right, shows a 'Mask Generator' block containing a blue 'Mamba Block' and a sigma (σ) symbol, which takes inputs μ and ε, and produces μ'. This block filters the trajectory sequence T1-T6, marking some trajectories with red Xs. The filtered output is then fed into another 'Traj-Mamba Encoder', also red-bordered, which receives a 'Load Weight' signal from the first encoder. The output of this second encoder is used to compute a loss function, represented as 1/2(L_MEC + L_mask), shown in a green box, which connects back to the trajectory embedding. The entire diagram uses arrows to indicate data flow, with solid lines for direct connections and dashed lines for optional or auxiliary paths. All modules are clearly labeled with text inside or near them, and colors are used to distinguish functional groups: gray for encoders, red for trajectory-related modules, blue for Mamba blocks, and green for loss computation. The layout follows a top-to-bottom processing flow, with parallel pathways merging at the final embedding stage."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents two diagrams, labeled A and B, illustrating a neural network architecture for predicting brain responses from image inputs using a transformer-based model. Diagram A shows the overall brain encoder architecture, while Diagram B details the cross-attention mechanism used within it.

In Diagram A, the process begins with "Image patches" at the bottom left, represented as a grid of yellow squares. These patches feed into an "Image Backbone" module, depicted as a light gray rectangle with rounded corners. Inside this module, there is a "Feedforward" block (light beige rectangle) and a "Self Attention" block (light pink rectangle), both connected by circular nodes indicating data flow. The entire backbone is labeled "x N", suggesting it is repeated N times. To the right of the backbone, "Positional encoding" (a small circle with a wavy line) is shown feeding into the backbone’s input. The output of the backbone is then passed to a "Brain responses" module, also a light gray rectangle with rounded corners. This module contains a "Feedforward" block (light beige) and a "Cross Attention" block (light green). The "Cross Attention" block has three inputs: "Q" (query), "K" (key), and "V" (value), which are visually indicated by arrows pointing to the block. Below the "Cross Attention" block, "Roi queries" (colored squares: red, blue, purple, green) are shown feeding into the "Q" input. The "Brain responses" module outputs through a "Linear" layer (white rectangle with a flame icon), which produces the final brain response prediction.

Diagram B provides a detailed view of the "Cross Attention" mechanism. On the left, "Roi query" (a cyan square) is shown, which feeds into a "Scaled dot product" block (white rectangle). This block takes "Q" (from the Roi query) and "K" (from "Image tokens," represented as a grid of yellow squares) as inputs. The output of the scaled dot product is "Attention scores" (a grid of gray squares). These scores are then multiplied element-wise with "V" (from "Image tokens"), which is fed into a circular node. The result of this multiplication passes through a "Linear" layer (white rectangle) and finally outputs "Roi vertices" (a cyan square). Additionally, "Positional encoding" (a small circle with a wavy line) is shown feeding into the "K" input of the scaled dot product. The entire mechanism is designed to allow learned queries for each Region of Interest (ROI) to selectively attend to relevant image tokens, thereby routing only pertinent information to predict the vertices in the corresponding ROI."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the Tree of Preferences for Diversified Recommendation (ToPRec), a method that infers user preferences through a hierarchical tree structure and integrates them with observed interactions to generate diverse recommendations. On the left, a user profile labeled 'Alice' is presented with her nickname, age (24), gender (female), bio ('peaks unclimbed, seas uncharted. My compass hungers.'), and reposts/likes (e.g., 'Summer escape...', 'Rail Travel...', '8hr 40min: Surviving on the longest flight...'). This profile feeds into a central 'Root' node, represented by a teal circular icon with a spiral pattern, symbolizing the starting point of preference inference. From the Root, three main branches extend: 'Daily Life' (blue rectangle), 'Leisure' (blue rectangle), and 'Arts' (brown rectangle). These represent broad preference categories inferred from Alice's profile. 'Daily Life' further branches into 'Transport' (blue), 'Cuisine' (gray), and 'Urban' (gray); 'Leisure' branches into 'Traveling' (blue) and 'Nature' (blue); 'Arts' branches into 'Visual' (brown) and 'Photography Tips' (brown). The diagram uses dashed arrows to indicate 'Implicit preference' (from 'Arts' to 'Visual') and 'Observed preference' (from 'Root' to 'Daily Life' and 'Leisure'), while solid arrows denote direct hierarchical relationships within the tree. Each branch eventually leads to 'Leaf' nodes (e.g., 'Airborne', 'Nature', 'Photography Tips'), representing specific, granular preference topics. From these leaf nodes, items are extracted and processed through two stages: 'Tree construction' (indicated by an arrow from 'Visual' to 'Photography Tips') and 'Item matching' (represented by a teal spiral icon). The output of item matching includes 'Cand. Items' (candidate items, shown as blue rectangles in a circle) and 'Obs. Inter.' (observed interactions, also blue rectangles). A separate process labeled 'Relevance' connects candidate items to 'Gen. Inter.' (generalized interactions, shown as blue rectangles in a circle), indicating how relevance is computed. Finally, 'Diversity' is applied to the combined set of candidate items and generalized interactions, resulting in a final set of recommended items. The entire process is designed to synthesize user-item interactions that consider both diversity and relevance, enabling the recommender system to provide diversified suggestions based on inferred preferences."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the TruthOverTricks framework, designed to evaluate misinformation detection systems under two primary types of shortcut learning: intrinsic shortcut induction and extrinsic shortcut injection. The central title \"TruthOverTricks: Evaluate Shortcut Learning in Misinformation Detection\" is positioned at the top, above a gauge-like icon symbolizing evaluation metrics. Below this, a central gray box labeled \"Misinformation Benchmarks\" serves as the core data source, connected bidirectionally to two main processing modules: \"Intrinsic Shortcut Induction\" on the left and \"Extrinsic Shortcut Induction\" on the right. These modules are visually distinct: the left module features an icon of a magnifying glass over documents, labeled \"Indicator Classifier,\" while the right module displays a robot icon labeled \"LLM Injector.\" Both modules are connected to the central benchmarks box via arrows indicating data flow. On the left side of the diagram, four vertical dashed boxes represent different types of potential shortcut features: Sentiment (with icons for positive, negative, neutral), Style (subjective, neutral), Topic (sports, crime, politics), and Perplexity (high, low). Each feature box has a small arrow pointing toward the \"Intrinsic Shortcut Induction\" module, suggesting these features are used to induce or identify spurious correlations within existing benchmarks. On the right side, three vertical dashed boxes represent different types of shortcut injection methods: \"Vanilla\" (a flower icon with text \"Please rewrite it to make it more convincing\"), \"Explicit\" (a mechanical device icon with text \"Make the {word choice} of the rewritten content {simple}\"), and \"Implicit\" (a person icon with text \"You are {elderly}. Please rewrite this passage\"). Each of these injection methods is connected by an arrow to the \"Extrinsic Shortcut Induction\" module, indicating that they are used to adversarially modify content to test detector robustness. The entire layout is structured to show a clear workflow: features on the left feed into intrinsic induction, while injection methods on the right feed into extrinsic injection, both ultimately interacting with the central benchmarks to evaluate detector performance."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates a multi-modal transformer architecture for vision-language tasks, specifically segmentation, multi-mask segmentation, and detection, processing an input image and text prompt together. The global layout is vertically structured, with two parallel input streams on the left — an 'Input Image' (x_v) and 'Input Texts' (x_t) — feeding into a central 'Multi-Modal Transformer'. This transformer processes both modalities and outputs results in three distinct modes labeled (a), (b), and (c). Each mode is visually separated by horizontal dashed lines. The transformer's internal structure is shown as a vertical stack of layers, each containing a placeholder for image feature h_v or text token h_t. The outputs from these layers feed into specific modules for each task. For (a) Segmentation, the image features are compared via dot product with a single mask token embedding e_m, which is highlighted in orange. A threshold (>0) determines if the mask is activated, producing an output mask M (16x16) that is interpolated to a final mask (448x448). In (b) Seg with multiple mask tokens, N=4 mask token embeddings (e_m1 to e_m4, colored pink, green, blue, yellow respectively) are used. Their dot products with image features are combined, again passing a >0 threshold, to produce an output mask M_up (32x32) interpolated to final mask (448x448). The caption notes N=4 is used in practice, though N=2 is illustrated. For (c) Detection, the output is a text sequence generated by the transformer, such as 'It is located at <Box>56,4,96,122</Box>', which is then applied to highlight a bounding box around the detected object in the original image. The arrows indicate data flow from inputs through the transformer to the respective outputs. All components are clearly labeled, including the 'Image Tokenizer' and 'Text Tokenizer' preprocessing steps, and the transformer's internal state representations."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates a neural network architecture designed for adaptive mesh deformation, titled 'The proposed mesh movement network'. The overall layout is a left-to-right pipeline with feedback loops, starting from an initial mesh and ending with an adaptive mesh containing the solution. The process is divided into distinct stages: input preparation, node patch extraction and normalization, graph transformer processing, adaptive mesh generation, and final output.

On the far left, an 'Initial mesh with the solution' (a red-to-blue gradient mesh) feeds into a vertical stack labeled 'Node patches', which contains N nodes (Node 1 to Node N), each represented as a small triangular mesh with vertices. Red arrows indicate '0-1 Normalization' applied to these patches, transforming them into 'Normalized patches' on the right side of the same vertical stack. These normalized patches are then fed into a central processing unit labeled 'Graph Transformer'. This unit consists of a 'Node Encoder' (green box), an 'Edge Encoder' (yellow box), and a sequence of 'Deform block' modules (gray boxes) connected vertically. A blue arrow labeled 'y' points to the top of the Graph Transformer, indicating input coordinates. The output of the last 'Deform block' connects to a 'Node decoder' (orange box), which generates 'Adapted node patches' (a vertical stack of modified triangular meshes). These adapted patches are then used to construct an 'Adaptive mesh' (a fine-grained triangular mesh) via 'Delaunay Triangulation-based Interpolation' (a green box). Finally, this adaptive mesh is shown with the solution overlaid, labeled 'Adaptive mesh with solution'.

Feedback connections exist throughout the architecture: a loop from the 'Adaptive mesh' back to the 'Node patches' stage allows iterative refinement; another loop from the 'Adapted node patches' to the 'Node decoder' indicates repeated deformation steps; and a loop from the 'Graph Transformer' back to the 'Normalized patches' suggests iterative optimization. The entire process runs 'Up to E epochs', as indicated by text below the 'Node decoder'. Additionally, a 'Denormalization' step (blue arrow) is shown at the bottom left, suggesting post-processing of the final output. The figure uses color-coded boxes (green, yellow, gray, orange) to distinguish different functional components, and arrows (red for normalization, blue for denormalization, black for main data flow) to indicate directionality and type of transformation."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents an overview of the UI-Genie-RM model and its training data construction pipeline. The layout is divided into two main horizontal sections. The top section, titled "Training Data Construction," details how training samples are generated through three parallel processes: Rule-based Verification, Trajectory Corruption, and Hard Negative Mining. These processes feed into a reward evaluation module that computes both action-level and task-level rewards. The bottom section illustrates the input-output structure of the UI-Genie-RM model itself, showing how it takes four inputs — Task, Historical Context, Current Screenshot, and Candidate Actions — to produce outputs supervised by the aforementioned rewards.

In the top section, the "Rule-based Verification" module is on the left, depicted as a light gray box containing three icons with labels: "Type Alignment" (with a multi-colored icon), "Coordinate Accuracy" (with a coordinate grid icon), and "Semantic Consistency" (with a pencil-and-paper icon). To its right is the "Trajectory Corruption" module, also in a light gray box, which displays two screenshots of a map interface labeled "Chosen" (with a green checkmark) and "Reject" (with a red X). Further right is the "Hard Negative Mining" module, shown as a light gray box with a smartphone screen displaying colorful app icons and a list of actions: "Tap on the more options icon," "Search contacts & places," and "Swipe up to view more contacts." An arrow labeled "Action-level Reward" points from this module to a "Reward Evaluation" box on the far right, which contains three candidate actions: "Swipe Up" (red-bordered, marked with a red X), "Click (61, 953)" (green-bordered, marked with a green checkmark), and "Complete" (red-bordered, marked with a red X). A second arrow labeled "Task-level Reward" connects the "Reward Evaluation" box back to the "Hard Negative Mining" module, indicating bidirectional feedback or supervision.

The central dividing element is a dark gray banner with the text "UI-Genie-RM" and a flame emoji, visually separating the training data generation process from the model's operational structure. Below this banner, the bottom section shows the model's input components. On the far left is the "Task" module, a light gray box with a circular target icon, containing the text: "Share the Just Mercy book with my personal email address." Next to it is the "Historical Context" module, also light gray, with a clock icon, showing a sequence of steps: "Step 1: Open Play Books app," "Step 2: Click on the search bar on the top of the screen," followed by ellipses, then "Step t-2: Click on the three dots on the top right corner of the screen," and "Step t-1: Click on share option on the screen." Each step is accompanied by a corresponding screenshot of the Play Books app interface. To the right of this is the "Current Screenshot" module, a light gray box with a camera icon, displaying a screenshot of the app at the current state. Finally, on the far right is the "Candidate Actions" module, a light gray box with a document icon, listing three possible actions: "Swipe Up" (orange-bordered), "Click (61, 953)" (orange-bordered), and "Complete" (orange-bordered), each with a small dot below indicating additional options. The entire diagram uses consistent visual styling with rounded boxes, clear labels, and color-coded feedback indicators (green for correct/positive, red for incorrect/negative) to convey the model's learning mechanism."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates the UPCL framework, which comprises two main components: Unbiased Prototypes-guided Modality Enhancement (UPME) and Cluster Prototype Consistency Regularization (CPCR). The overall layout is divided into two parallel processing streams, one for 'Person' and one for 'Vehicle', each starting from 'Instances' and progressing through 'Features', 'Prototypes', 'Memory', 'Unbiased Memory', and finally to 'Category Memory'. Each stream has a corresponding color-coded pathway: blue for Person and yellow for Vehicle. The bottom legend defines symbols: RGB/NI/TI Features (light blue circle, light blue square, light blue triangle); RGB/NI/TI Prototypes (dark blue circle, dark blue square, dark blue triangle); Modality-Unbiased Prototypes (green striped hexagon, blue striped hexagon, purple striped hexagon); Category-Consistent Prototypes (blue cylinder, yellow cylinder, green cylinder). The left side shows input instances (thermal and RGB images) mapped to features (geometric shapes) and then to prototypes (colored shapes). These prototypes are stored in a 'Memory' module (blue box for Person, yellow box for Vehicle) and updated via a feedback loop from UPME. UPME, depicted as a circular module with arrows connecting modality-unbiased prototypes, aggregates them using Equation (9) to enhance cross-modal and multi-modal representations. From the 'Unbiased Memory' (red-bordered boxes for Person and Vehicle), prototypes are clustered (orange 'Cluster' boxes) and then transformed into category-consistent prototypes (cylinders) via CPCR, which uses these to regularize the model and improve category-wise decision boundaries. Arrows indicate data flow: from Instances → Features → Prototypes → Memory → Unbiased Memory → Category Memory, with bidirectional arrows for updates from UPME back to Memory. The figure also includes dashed arrows indicating the update mechanism from UPME to Memory, and solid arrows showing the forward propagation through clustering and CPCR."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents a two-part workflow diagram for the UniDomain system, divided into \"Learning\" (top) and \"Planning\" (bottom) phases. The top section details domain construction from a DROID Dataset through three sequential stages: (a) Keyframe Extraction, (b) Atomic Domain Generation, and (c) Domain Fusion. The bottom section illustrates the planning pipeline: (d) Problem Generation, (e) Planning via PDDL Solver, and (f) Execution, all triggered by a natural language task prompt. The entire diagram uses consistent visual metaphors: green boxes for learning components, purple for planning components, and white boxes for data or intermediate representations. Arrows indicate directional flow, while bidirectional arrows denote feedback loops. All modules are labeled with descriptive text and some include internal code-like syntax examples. The left side of the diagram is vertically labeled \"Learning\" and \"Planning\" to distinguish the two major phases. The \"Learning\" phase begins with a cylinder labeled \"DROID Dataset\" feeding into keyframe extraction, which outputs multiple frame grids. These feed into atomic domain generation, which includes a closed-loop refinement loop involving solvability check and solution verification. This stage produces atomic domains that are then fused into a unified domain and meta-domain. The \"Planning\" phase starts with a natural language task prompt shown in a text box, which triggers problem generation using the meta-domain. This involves predicate grouping, predicate & operator filtering, and problem generation. The resulting problem is fed into a PDDL solver, which outputs a sequence of actions. These actions are executed in real-world robotic scenarios, shown as a grid of images labeled (f) Execution. The diagram visually emphasizes modularity, feedback mechanisms, and the transition from learned domain knowledge to executable robot plans."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates the UTILGEN framework, a three-stage process for feedback-driven data augmentation, structured vertically with each stage separated by dashed lines. Stage I, "Task-Driven Data Valuation via Weight-Net," is at the top. It begins with two input boxes labeled "Training Data" and "Val Data," both containing image icons. These feed into a "Weight-Net" module (orange rectangle with a flame icon), which outputs a "Weight" value. A red arrow labeled "Step 3" points from Weight-Net to a "Classifier" (pink hexagonal grid with a flame icon), indicating evaluation with a loss term "L_eval." Another red arrow labeled "Step 2" points from Val Data to the Classifier, labeled "Evaluate." The Classifier's output feeds into a "L_train" term, which combines with "L_weighted" (from Weight-Net) via a multiplication symbol (×) to form a weighted loss. This weighted loss updates the Weight-Net (Step 3) and the Classifier (Step 1), forming a feedback loop. A gray arrow labeled "Stage II" points rightward from this stage to the next.

Stage II, "Model-Level Generation Capability Optimization," is enclosed in a red dashed box. It starts with a "Diffusion Model" (blue cylinder with a flame icon) that generates "Synthetic Data" (green-bordered box with image icons). This synthetic data flows into a "Weight-Net" (orange rectangle) that produces a "Utility Ranking" (bar chart with upward trend and trophy icon). The utility ranking then generates "Preference Data" (green-bordered box with image icons and trophy icon). A red arrow labeled "Direct Preference Optimization (DPO)" connects the preference data back to the Diffusion Model, indicating iterative optimization. A downward arrow labeled "Maximizing Task-Specific Utility Scores" links this stage to Stage III.

Stage III, "Instance-Level Generation Policy Optimization," is at the bottom. It begins with "Real Data" (green-bordered box with four image icons) feeding into a "Text Inversion" module (yellow rectangle). This module outputs "Learnable prompt embeddings" (four light-colored squares). These embeddings connect to a "Diffusion Model" (blue cylinder with a snowflake icon) that generates "Synthetic Data" (green-bordered box with image icons). The synthetic data then feeds into a "Weight-Net" (orange rectangle with a snowflake icon). A dashed line labeled "CFG¹ > CFG²" connects the Text Inversion module to an "Inversion" module (green rectangle), which receives "noise" (white circle labeled "noise" with a Gaussian distribution symbol "N(0, I)"). The Inversion module outputs "Golden noise" (yellow circle), which feeds back into the Diffusion Model. The entire Stage III is connected to Stage II via the "Maximizing Task-Specific Utility Scores" arrow, indicating a unified optimization goal across all stages."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates a 3D Gaussian-based neural rendering framework for image synthesis, structured into three main vertical sections: input processing, feature extraction, and multi-loss optimization. At the top, two 'Rasterizer' modules process a 3D Gaussian representation: one generates depth maps from the 'Source' view, while the other produces color images from the 'Reference' view. These outputs feed into downstream modules. The depth map is used to compute occlusion weight ω and visibility ν, which depend on camera parameters {K, M}. The color output is split into multiple channels: Normal, Distance, and Depth, each producing corresponding visualizations. These channels converge into a homography matrix H, which feeds into two loss functions, L_nc and L_ns, responsible for normal consistency and surface smoothness. The reference image I_r and source image I_s are processed through a feature extractor f, yielding feature maps F_r and F_s respectively. These features are compared via a pixel-wise loss L_p, which incorporates occlusion weight ω, visibility ν, and homography H. Additionally, a photometric loss L_I compares gradients ∇I and ∇Ĩ between ground truth and rendered images. A final feature alignment loss L_f measures similarity between F_s and F_r across spatial grids. All losses are combined to train the model end-to-end. The layout follows a top-down flow: 3D Gaussians → rasterization → multi-channel outputs → loss computation → feature alignment → joint optimization. Key components include blue boxes for rasterizers, yellow boxes for occlusion/visibility/homography, pink boxes for loss functions, and red arrows indicating loss dependencies. The entire architecture is designed to jointly optimize geometry, appearance, and feature consistency under varying viewpoints."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates a three-stage training pipeline for VITA-1.5, designed to progressively integrate vision and audio modalities into a large language model (LLM) while mitigating modality conflicts. The entire pipeline is horizontally segmented into three main stages: Stage 2 (Audio Input Tuning) and Stage 3 (Audio Output Tuning), with Stage 1 (Vision-Language Training) implied by context but not visually depicted. Each stage contains sub-stages with distinct components and data inputs.

Stage 2, labeled "Audio Input Tuning," is split into two sub-stages: Stage 2.1 (Audio Alignment) and Stage 2.2 (Audio SFT). In Stage 2.1, an LLM (represented by a gray rounded rectangle with a snowflake icon) receives inputs from a Speech Encoder (green waveform icon) and a Text input (yellow folder icon), along with a Task Guidance Token (orange dashed box). The LLM outputs are connected to another Speech Encoder, indicating alignment between speech and text representations. This stage uses "Speech-transcription pairs" as input data. Stage 2.2, "Audio SFT," features a larger LLM (gray rounded rectangle with a flame icon) that integrates multiple modalities: a Vision Adapter (white box) connected to a Visual Encoder (image and video icons), a Speech Adapter (white box) connected to a Speech Encoder (green waveform icon), and a Text input (yellow folder icon). The LLM processes these inputs to perform audio instruction fine-tuning. This stage uses "Speech/Text Caption (4%) & QA (20%)" data.

Stage 3, "Audio Output Tuning," is divided into Stage 3.1 (Codec Training) and Stage 3.2 (NAR + AR Decoder Training). Stage 3.1 trains a Codec model, which includes a Codec Encoder (red flame icon) and a Codec Decoder (red flame icon), both processing Speech inputs (green waveform icon). The output of the Codec Decoder feeds into an LLM Embedding module (blue snowflake icon) that also receives Text input (yellow folder icon). Stage 3.2 trains two decoders: an AR Speech Decoder (white box with red flame icon) and a NAR Speech Decoder (white box with red flame icon), both receiving input from the LLM Embedding module. This stage uses "Text-Speech Data" as input. All modules are connected via arrows indicating data flow, with some connections showing bidirectional interaction. The diagram uses consistent visual cues: LLMs are gray boxes with icons, encoders are gray trapezoids, adapters are white boxes, and decoders are white boxes with red flame icons. The overall layout progresses left-to-right, reflecting the sequential nature of the training pipeline."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure is divided into two main sections labeled (a) Dynamic Token Selection and (b) Adaptive Token Caching, illustrating a two-step methodology for accelerating a Vision-Language Action (VLA) model's decoding process across timesteps. In section (a), the top-left shows 'Patch Similarity' between two images, leading to 'Static Token Selection' (i), where a static token representation p_static is derived. This transitions to 'Evicting Task-Relevant Tokens' (ii), where tokens are selectively removed. Below this, a detailed flowchart begins with a 'VLA Decoder' feeding into an 'Attention Matrix'. The matrix contains tokens represented by colored squares: blue for static tokens and green for text tokens. A 'Mean' operation computes a task-relevant score, which then selects tokens from a 'Dynamic Pool' (represented by orange squares) for eviction or update. The 'Static Pool' (blue squares) retains static tokens, while the 'Dynamic Pool' holds task-relevant tokens. An arrow labeled 'Evict' points from the dynamic pool to the static pool, indicating token eviction, while an arrow labeled 'Update' points back to the dynamic pool, suggesting token updates. The bottom of section (a) shows a 'Select' operation choosing tokens from the dynamic pool. Section (b) illustrates 'Adaptive Token Caching' across two timesteps: T-1 and T. Each timestep has a VLA decoder stack with multiple layers (Layer 1 to Layer N). At timestep T-1, the VLA layers show token representations with some tokens marked as X (evicted) and others retained. At timestep T, the same structure is shown but with updated token states. Between the timesteps, an arrow labeled 'Next Step' indicates progression. A dashed yellow arrow labeled 'Attention Entropy' connects the two timesteps, showing how entropy values α¹%, α²%, ..., αᴺ% are computed per layer, reflecting the adaptability of token caching. These entropy values feed into a 'Static Cache Pool', which is updated and used to prefill the VLA decoder at timestep T. The static cache pool is depicted as a box containing blue squares representing cached static tokens. The entire figure uses a clean layout with clear arrows and labels to depict the workflow of dynamic token selection and adaptive caching."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents a three-part architectural diagram of a reinforcement learning-driven visual iterative reasoning framework. Part (a) illustrates the core RL loop: starting with 'Instructions + Human Query', the system interacts with an external environment via a 'Rollout Module' containing a 'Search Engine' and a 'Policy Model (VLMs)', generating trajectories (Trjs: H1, H2, ..., H6). These trajectories feed into a 'Reference Model' and a 'Reward Func (RAG Specific)' defined by the equation r = α·r_pat + β·r_ans + γ·r_ret. The reward output (r1, r2, ..., r6) undergoes 'Group Computation' to produce advantages (Adv1, Adv2, ...), which are used to update the policy model via KL divergence minimization. Part (b) details the 'Visual Perception Action Space', showing how a user interface (e.g., a webpage with tables and charts) is processed through 'Pre-defined Visual Perception Actions' — including selecting, cropping, and zooming — to focus on a 'Region of Interest'. This enables coarse-to-fine visual analysis. Part (c) describes the 'Fine-grained Retrieval-based Reward' mechanism. It begins with trajectories (H1, H2, ...) feeding into 'Answer Extraction', followed by 'Rule-based' and 'LLM-based Evaluation' for outcome judgment ('True or False'). Two reward components are computed: 'Pattern Reward' (based on pattern/action verification) and 'Model-based Reward' (from LLM evaluation). These combine with 'Retrieval-based Reward', which evaluates stacked retrieval results from multiple rounds (Round 1 to N) against a 'Golden Image' versus 'Unrelated' images using the NDCG metric. All three parts are visually distinct, with part (a) in light blue and peach tones, part (b) in white and blue, and part (c) in green and blue, using arrows to indicate data flow and feedback loops."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the Venus-MAXWELL framework pipeline, which uses a single forward-backward computation to train a pre-trained language model (PLM) to predict the mutation landscape of a protein based on its wild-type sequence and experimental ΔΔG data. The layout is organized into two parallel input streams converging on a joint loss calculation. On the top stream, a wild-type amino acid (AA) sequence is encoded into one-hot encodings, fed into the PLM to produce an AA distribution matrix. This distribution undergoes a Hadamard product with a replication of the mutation landscape matrix, followed by column summation to yield a predicted mutation landscape. The bottom stream processes a table of mutations and their experimental ΔΔG values into a mutation landscape matrix, where masked regions indicate absence of experimental data. The predicted mutation landscape from the top stream is subtracted element-wise from the ground truth mutation landscape from the bottom stream, and the resulting difference is used to compute the joint loss, which serves as the training objective. All components are connected via arrows indicating data flow, with clear labels for each module, including color-coded matrices (green for positive, dark green for negative, yellow for masked), and mathematical notation such as ℝ^(n×|A|) to denote matrix dimensions. The entire pipeline is enclosed within a dashed-line boundary labeled 'Framework pipeline'."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates the pipeline of Vgent, a framework for long-context video understanding using a graph-based retrieval-reasoning-augmented generation approach. The entire process is divided into two main sections: \"Offline Graph Construction\" on the left and \"Retrieval-Reasoning Augmented Generation\" on the right, connected by arrows indicating data flow. In the left section, multiple video clips (represented as small images) feed into a stage labeled \"Subtitles,\" which then passes data to an \"LVLM\" (Large Vision-Language Model) module shown as a light blue rectangle. The LVLM outputs a JSON-like structure containing entities and descriptions. Below this, a \"Entity Merging and Node Connection\" step processes these entities, visually represented as green squares (video clips), pink triangles, and blue triangles (entities), connected by lines to form a graph structure. This constructed graph is passed to the right section. On the right side, under the heading \"Retrieval-Reasoning Augmented Generation,\" a question is posed: \"Please identify the option that corresponds to the order of events as they occur in the video.\" The first step is \"Query Keywords Extraction,\" which extracts keywords like \"zumba,\" \"clean,\" \"milking cow,\" and \"playing trombone\" from the question. These keywords lead to \"Structured Query Refinement,\" generating specific queries such as Q1: \"Is 'zumba' shown in the video?\" and Q4: \"Is 'trombone' shown in the video?\" These queries are used in a \"Graph-based Clip Retrieval\" step, which uses a table showing clips (3, 4, 5, 11, 13) and their corresponding query responses (checkmarks or Xs). Clips 3, 4, 11, and 13 are selected as \"Refined Clips\" based on positive responses. These refined clips are then fed into an \"Information Aggregation\" box, which states the inferred temporal order: \"Milking cow is shown first, followed by zumba, then clean, and finally playing trombone.\" This aggregated information is passed to a second \"LVLM\" module at the bottom, which generates the final answer: \"milking cow --> zumba --> clean and jerk --> playing trombone.\" The entire diagram uses a clean layout with directional arrows guiding the workflow from left to right and top to bottom, with distinct color coding for modules (light blue for LVLM, peach for processing steps, white for query tables) and clear text labels for each component."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents the ViSpec framework, divided into two main sections by a vertical dashed line: "Target Model" on the left and "Draft Model" on the right. The Target Model is a complete vision-language transformer architecture, while the Draft Model is a simplified version designed for efficient inference. Both models share similar components but differ in how they handle visual inputs.

In the Target Model, at the bottom, there are four image patches shown as small dog images, labeled as v0, v1, v2, v3. These feed into a blue-bordered "Vision Encoder" module, which outputs corresponding visual embeddings v0, v1, v2, v3. Adjacent to this is an "Embedding" module that processes text tokens such as "This", "is", "a", producing text embeddings eThis, eis, ea. These embeddings are concatenated with the visual embeddings and passed upward through multiple "Decoder Layers" (blue boxes with snowflake icons), culminating in an "LM Head" (blue box with snowflake icon) that generates final output tokens like "is", "a", "dog". The text tokens "This", "is", "a" are also shown above the LM Head, indicating the prompt.

The Draft Model, on the right, uses a "Vision Adaptor" (orange box) to compress the same visual embeddings v0, v1, v2, v3 into a smaller set of compressed image tokens cv. This adaptor is connected to the Vision Encoder via a dotted orange arrow. The compressed tokens cv are then fed into the first "Decoder Layer" (orange box) of the draft model, along with text embeddings eThis, eis, ea (from the embedding module) and a global visual feature vector gv (yellow boxes). The Decoder Layer contains an "MLP" (orange box) that processes these inputs. The output of the MLP is split into fThis, fis, fa (green boxes), which are then passed to the next "LM Head" (blue box with snowflake icon). The text token "dog" is shown above this LM Head, indicating the generated output. The process continues with subsequent decoder layers, each receiving the previous layer's output and generating new text tokens, as indicated by the ellipsis (...). The global visual feature vector gv is also shown being injected into the text generation process, connecting to the MLP in each decoder layer. The figure uses color coding: blue for target model components, orange for draft model components, green for hidden states, yellow for global visual features, and gray for text tokens or labels."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents the framework of VideoMAR, divided into two main sections: (a) Framework Overview and (b) Efficient Training. In section (a), the top row displays four video frames labeled s1 through s4, each represented by rectangular blocks. These blocks are color-coded: green for 'visible', gray for 'masked', and cyan for 'predicted'. Below this, a central blue box labeled 'VideoMAR' describes it as 'Frame-wise causal & Intra-frame bidirectional'. Beneath VideoMAR is another blue box labeled 'Continuous Video VAE', which shows four corresponding visual frames s1-s4, each depicting a waterfall scene. To the right of VideoMAR, a dashed blue-bordered box titled 'Next-frame diffusion loss' contains four rows of blocks matching s1-s4, with red Xs on s1, s2, and s4, and a green checkmark on s3, indicating loss calculation. Below this, a yellow-dashed bordered box labeled 'Attention mask' shows a 4x4 grid where the top-left quadrant is filled with orange squares, representing the causal attention mask. Section (b) is split into two vertical panels: 'Stage 1' on the left and 'Stage 2' on the right. Stage 1 is labeled 'Temporal short-to-long' and shows an upward arrow with a sequence T: 5 → 13 → 25, indicating increasing temporal resolution. Stage 2 is labeled 'Spatial progressive-res' and shows an upward arrow with a sequence 256×256 → 480×768, indicating increasing spatial resolution. The entire layout is structured to show the training pipeline from frame representation to loss computation and then to progressive training stages."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure is divided into two horizontal rows by a dashed line, each illustrating a distinct phase of the method. The top row details the construction of a ViewPoint Map from either a CubeMap or an Equirectangular Map. On the left, a CubeMap is shown composed of six square panels arranged in a cross, each displaying a portion of a landscape scene. A red dashed rectangle labeled 'F' highlights a specific region on one panel. An arrow labeled 'project' with a transformation notation 'r → r^a' indicates projecting this region onto a target area. Below, an Equirectangular Map is shown as a flat, rectangular panorama with the same region 'F' marked. A diagonal dashed line separates these two representations. To the right, a ViewPoint Map is depicted as a diamond-shaped image with a red dashed rectangle 'F' overlaid, showing the projected region. Between the CubeMap and ViewPoint Map is a small diagram illustrating a remap process: a grid of nine colored squares (U, L, D, R, C, etc.) with a central red-dashed square 'F' is transformed via a 'remap' arrow into the diamond-shaped ViewPoint Map. The bottom row describes the main processing pipeline. On the left, an 'Input' image (the ViewPoint Map) is shown above a 'Projected Input Perspective Video' (a black rectangle with a white diamond shape) and a 'Mask' (a black rectangle with a white diamond shape). These three components are connected by arrows labeled 'add noise' and 'concat', indicating they are concatenated along the channel dimension. This concatenated tensor feeds into a large green box labeled 'Pano Attention', which contains an 'Attn Block' with a small grid of icons (checkmark, arrow, etc.). From there, the output connects to another green box labeled 'Perspective Attention', which contains multiple 'Attn Block' units, each receiving a small perspective video frame as input and producing an output. A curved arrow labeled 'Prompt' loops back from the 'Perspective Attention' block to the 'Pano Attention' block, with a multiplication symbol '× N' indicating repetition. The final output is a diamond-shaped image labeled 'Output', visually similar to the ViewPoint Map but refined through the attention mechanisms. The entire diagram uses consistent color coding: blue for maps, green for attention blocks, and black for structural elements."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the architecture of VisualQuality-R1, a reinforcement learning-based system for evaluating relative visual quality. The layout is divided into two parallel top-level branches, each processing a different input image (xi and xj) with the same text prompt C. Each branch feeds into a central 'VisualQuality-R1' module, represented by a robot icon labeled with the policy πθ. From this module, K sequential responses are generated, shown as stacked rectangular boxes labeled 'Response 1' through 'Response K'. Each response contains a <think> section with a reasoning explanation and an <answer> section with a numerical score. For example, Response 1 for xi yields score 1.20, while Response K for xj yields score 4.20. These K scores for each image are collected into a vertical stack labeled 'Predicted Scores', which then feeds into a 'Thurstone Model' box. The Thurstone Model computes an asymmetric comparative probability based on the difference between the k-th score of xi and the mean score of xj, standardized by their respective variances. This probability is then used to compute a 'Fidelity Reward'. A separate 'Human Preference' box provides ground-truth feedback, which is also fed into the 'Fidelity Reward' box. The entire system is framed as 'Reinforcement Learning to Rank (RL2R)', indicating that the policy πθ is optimized using this reward signal. The diagram uses color coding: gray for xi's responses, blue for xj's responses, and black for the evaluation components. Arrows indicate the flow of data from inputs to outputs, including the feedback loop from Human Preference to Fidelity Reward."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents a four-stage neurosymbolic learning pipeline for generating code rules from agent trajectories, structured as four vertically aligned rectangular blocks labeled Stage 1 through Stage 4, arranged in two columns. The top section contains the title \"NeuroSymbolic Learning of Code Rules\". Stage 1, titled \"Exploration of Trajectory Data,\" is on the left. It shows two horizontal rows: the top row labeled \"Real Trajectory\" contains circular nodes labeled a₀, o₁, a₁, o₂, ..., oₜ, connected by arrows indicating sequence. Below it, the \"Predicted Trajectory\" row has similarly shaped nodes a₀, δ₁, a₁, δ₂, ..., δₜ, with green checkmarks above some action nodes (a₀, a₁) and red Xs above some observation nodes (o₂, oₜ), indicating correct/incorrect predictions. Stage 2, \"Learning Symbolic Knowledge,\" is directly above Stage 4 and to the right of Stage 1. It contains three parallel rectangular boxes: \"Action Rules\" (white background, describing conditions like 'if player does not have enough materials...'), \"Knowledge Graph\" (white background, showing example JSON-like structure with nodes and edges), and \"Scene Graph\" (white background, showing another JSON-like structure with entities like 'sheep', 'zombie', 'sand'). These are fed by an LLM box (pink rectangle) which receives input from \"Real trajectory t-k:t\" and \"oₜ in the real trajectory\" (dashed lines). Stage 3, \"Translating Symbolic Knowledge to Code,\" is below Stage 2 and to the right of Stage 1. It begins with a prompt box (orange background) containing text: \"Prompt: Implement the learned rules in Python function to evaluate an observation and action, optionally using the Knowledge Graph or Scene Graph as extra arguments. Input: [textual rules, knowledge_graph, scene_graph].\" This connects via an arrow to an LLM box (pink rectangle), which then connects via an arrow to an output box (white background) displaying sample Python code for functions def CodeRule_0(...) and def CodeRule_i(...). Stage 4, \"Code Rule Set Pruning via Maximum Set Coverage,\" is at the bottom left. It displays three overlapping ellipses: a large yellow ellipse labeled \"CodeRule_1\", a medium-sized yellow ellipse labeled \"CodeRule_2\", and a small gray ellipse labeled \"CodeRule_i\". Inside these ellipses are colored dots: cyan dots represent transitions that can be predicted by the world model, and pink dots represent transitions that cannot be predicted. A legend at the bottom left explains this: a yellow circle with a cyan dot indicates \"Regions covered by selected/dropped CodeRules\" and a yellow circle with a pink dot indicates \"Transitions can/cannot be predicted by world model.\" Arrows connect the output of Stage 3 to the input of Stage 4, indicating the flow of generated code rules into the pruning stage. The entire figure uses a light green background, with each stage enclosed in a rounded rectangle with a darker green border. All text is black except for highlighted portions in orange or within code blocks."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the FlashMMR framework, divided into three main sections: Feature Extraction, Feature Fusion, and Multi-Scale Temporal Prediction, with an additional Post-Verification module for training. On the left, under \"Feature Extraction\", a video input is processed by a Video Encoder (white box) to produce a sequence of video features. Simultaneously, a Text Query (e.g., \"Two children... homework\") is fed into a Text Encoder (white box) to generate text features. These text features are combined with Dummy Tokens (D1 to D_Ld, shown as blue rounded rectangles) from a Dummy Encoder (white box) to form a key (K) sequence. The video features serve as the query (Q) and the text features as the value (V) in the subsequent fusion stage. The central section, labeled \"Feature Fusion\" in a green background, contains two modules: Adaptive Cross-Attention (white box) and Fusion Feature Encoder (white box). The Adaptive Cross-Attention takes Q and V as inputs and outputs a set of fused features {f_i}_{i=1}^{L_v}, represented as purple rounded rectangles arranged vertically, with varying color intensities indicating semantic relevance. These fused features are then passed to the Fusion Feature Encoder. The right section, \"Multi-Scale Temporal Prediction\" in a gray background, receives the fused features and processes them through a Temporal Feature Layering Module and an ASR Module. Outputs include Boundary Prediction and Confidence Scores {c_i}_{i=1,2,3}. These predictions are formatted as triplets {(s_i, e_i, c_i)}_{i=1}^3, shown as dashed boxes. Below this, the Post-Verification module (purple background) refines these predictions during training. It takes the fused features and the predicted triplets as inputs, generating a Feature Refinement Unit output and a self-similarity matrix S_i (grid of purple squares) and a guide vector T_i (another grid). These are compared against Ground Truth Moments B* (blue and light blue bars labeled s_1*, e_1*, ..., s_3*, e_3*) to compute a Temporal IoU score, which supervises the refinement process. Arrows indicate data flow: from video/text inputs to encoders, to fusion modules, to prediction modules, and back to the post-verification module for refinement. The entire structure is designed to extract, fuse, predict, and refine temporal boundaries in video based on text queries."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure presents the architecture of SAVG-DETR, divided into three main components labeled (a), (b), and (c), each enclosed in a light gray rounded rectangle. Component (a) is titled 'Aerial Video-Text Feature Extractor' and contains two parallel green modules: 'Visual Encoder' on the left and 'Language Encoder' below it. The Visual Encoder takes multiple aerial video frames as input, represented by stacked images with a green bus annotation pointing to one frame. It outputs visual features S5, S4, S3, shown as horizontal stacked rectangles. The Language Encoder processes text input and outputs a linearized vector. These two streams converge into a 'Cross-scale Visual-only Fusion Branch' (light blue box) which fuses visual features across scales. Below this, an 'Intra-scale Multi-modality Interaction Branch' (also light blue) integrates visual and language features at each scale using color-coded tokens (blue for visual, green for language, pink for frame). Both branches feed into a 'Multi-level Language Modulation' module (green box) which generates object queries and position embeddings via a 'Query and Position Generator' (orange box). These queries are then passed to the decoder in component (c). Component (c) is titled 'Hierarchical Progressive Decoder' and contains three vertical purple boxes labeled 'High-level Decoder', 'Mid-level Decoder', and 'Low-level Decoder', each receiving inputs from the query generator and corresponding feature maps (high-level, mid-level, low-level). Each decoder progressively refines predictions, culminating in a 'Predicted Tube' output shown as a sequence of aerial images. The bottom section, labeled (b.1) and (b.2), details the intra-scale interaction mechanism. (b.1) shows a temporal interaction layer (light green) followed by a spatial interaction layer (light yellow), processing video tokens, language tokens, and frame tokens over T frames. (b.2) illustrates the cross-scale fusion branch, where features F5, F4, F3 from different scales are fused through multiple 'Fusion' blocks (yellow boxes) with FD and SAC modules, and finally processed by MSFF-FE (green box) before being fed into the decoder. All connections are depicted with arrows indicating data flow direction, and feature maps are represented as vertical dotted lines with colored dots indicating different modalities or levels."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates the architecture of PEVA, a video diffusion model, divided into three main sections labeled (a), (b), and (c). Section (a) shows \"Random Time Skips\" where a filmstrip of video frames is processed. On the left, two stylized human motion figures are shown against a checkerboard background, indicating pose or action data. The filmstrip contains three video frames, each connected by green arrows to an \"Encoder\" block. These encoders convert the video frames into feature representations. Section (b) details \"Auto Regressive Training\" using a DiT block (xM), which is a transformer-based architecture. The encoded tokens from section (a) are fed into this block. The DiT block consists of multiple stacked layers, each containing blue square blocks representing transformer layers, with some gray squares indicating masked or noise tokens. Red diagonal arrows show self-attention mechanisms within the image, while orange arrows indicate cross-attention from noisy tokens to clean tokens from previous frames. A red \"loss\" box at the top connects back to the DiT block via red arrows, indicating the training loop with loss computation. Below the DiT block, labels identify components: \"clean + noise tokens\", \"attn. cattn. ffn fc\" (attention, cross-attention, feed-forward network, fully connected layer), and \"DiT block (xM)\". Section (c) describes \"Conditioning Types\". It includes two small diagrams showing attention mechanisms: one labeled \"within image attn.\" with a 3x3 grid of cells, each marked with \"1\", and another labeled \"cattn. to context\" with a 2x2 grid. An orange arrow points from these diagrams to an \"MLP\" (Multi-Layer Perceptron) block, which is then connected to a \"Per Frame AdaLN\" module. The MLP computes parameters (shift_t, scale_t, gate_t) as a function of time t, which are applied via AdaLN layers to condition the model on per-frame actions or contexts. The entire diagram uses color coding: green for encoder connections, red for loss feedback, orange for cross-attention, and blue for transformer layers. Text labels are clear and positioned near relevant components. The layout flows logically from input encoding (a) to transformer processing (b) to conditioning (c), illustrating the end-to-end training and inference pipeline."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the DCBoost framework, which consists of three main components: an Online Network, a Target Network, and an Inference module, all interconnected through data flow and parameter updates. On the left, two input images — one showing a snow leopard and another showing a bus — feed into separate transformation functions T¹(·) and T²(·), both represented as red-bordered boxes. These transformed inputs enter the Online Network (orange dashed rectangle) via f₀(·), which generates a latent representation z₀. This z₀ is then passed through a noise injection mechanism modeled as N(0, σ²I), symbolized by a circular node with an arrow pointing down, before being processed by function g(·). The output of g(·) is further transformed by g(z₀) and contributes to the total loss L_pos + L_neg, which is computed alongside the instance consistency loss L_ins. The Target Network (blue dashed rectangle) receives input from T²(·) via f_t(·), which shares weights with the inference branch’s f_t(·) as indicated by a bidirectional arrow labeled 'Share'. The Target Network also receives z_t, which is derived from z₀ via a moving average operation, shown as a yellow downward arrow labeled 'Moving Average' connecting f₀(·) to f_t(·). The inference branch, located at the bottom, includes a k-means clustering module (black box with colorful cluster icons) that processes the output of f_t(·) and feeds into an Adaptive k-NN Filtering module (black rounded rectangle containing a globe with colored points representing clusters). The filtering module outputs pseudo-labels for all samples at the end of each epoch. All arrows indicate directional data or gradient flow, while the dotted lines denote shared or averaged parameters. The overall structure emphasizes online learning with EMA-based parameter updates and adaptive filtering for pseudo-label generation."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The diagram illustrates the ZEBRA framework, divided into two main phases: Training and Inference. The Training phase is split into two parallel pathways: Subject-Invariant Feature Extraction (left) and Semantic-Specific Feature Extraction (right), both processing fMRI data from Train Subjects. The Inference phase (bottom) uses only the invariant pathway for zero-shot generalization on Test Unseen Subjects.

In Training, fMRI data from train subjects flows into a ViT (Vision Transformer) module, producing a feature representation E. This E splits into two branches: E_s (subject-invariant) and E_i (subject-specific). E_s feeds into a Vision Projector P_i(·), while E_i feeds into another Vision Projector P_s(·). The output F_i from P_i(·) is processed by a GRL (Gradient Reversal Layer) before being compared to semantic labels via loss L_Fspe. The output F_s from P_s(·) is used to reconstruct fMRI via a Decoder with reconstruction loss L_rec. A Difference module computes the difference between E_s and E_i, which is fed back to adjust the ViT. The subject-specific branch also connects to a CLS module with classification loss L_Ecls and a DIS module with domain adaptation loss L_Edis. The semantic-specific branch outputs F, which is aligned with visual stimuli and image captions through L_prior. The entire training process is governed by multiple losses: L_Ecls, L_Edis, L_Fspe, L_prior, and L_rec.

During Inference, the process is simplified. An unseen subject’s fMRI data enters a ViT, producing E. Only the subject-invariant branch E_s is used, passed through P_i(·) to produce F_i. F_i then feeds into a Diffusion Prior module, which generates reconstructed images via SDXL. The final output is a set of reconstructed images, demonstrating zero-shot generalization capability. The diagram visually emphasizes the separation of subject-invariant and semantic-specific features, with clear directional arrows indicating data flow and loss feedback loops. All modules are labeled with their functions, and the color-coding helps distinguish between different stages and types of features."
}

{
  "fig_desc": "The figure illustrates the Zero-Shot Neural Compression Denoiser (ZS-NCD) framework, divided into two main phases: "Zero-shot Learning Phase" and "Zero-shot Denoising Phase", separated by horizontal lines. The top section shows the training phase, while the bottom section shows the inference phase.

In the top section, on the left, there is a single noisy image labeled "single noisy image: y", depicted as a pixelated, colorful image with a green square highlighting a region. This image feeds into a set of overlapping patches, labeled "set of overlapping patches as input for training: P(i,j)(y)", represented as a grid of small square patches with ellipses indicating continuation. These patches are fed into a central module labeled "Neural Compression", which has a gray rectangular box with rounded corners, containing an internal structure resembling a compressor-decompressor with a vertical bar and a label "rate constraint" above it. An arrow points from the patch grid to this module. From the Neural Compression module, an arrow points to another grid labeled "set of overlapping patches as target for training: P(i,j)(y)", which visually matches the input grid but appears slightly cleaner or more structured, suggesting the compressed-reconstructed targets. This entire top section represents the learning phase where the model learns to compress and reconstruct patches from a single noisy image.

The bottom section, labeled "Zero-shot Learning Phase", begins with a large noisy image labeled "P(i,j)(y)" with a black square highlighting a region, similar to the top image but with different coloration. To its right, a vertical list labeled "batch of all patches" shows a sequence of small square patches with ellipses, representing the input patches for denoising. These patches feed into the same "Neural Compression" module (identical in appearance to the one above), which outputs a vertical list labeled "reconstructed patches", showing smoother, less noisy versions of the input patches. An arrow points from the batch of patches to the module, and another arrow points from the module to the reconstructed patches. This section demonstrates how the learned model processes a new batch of patches to produce reconstructions.

Finally, the bottom-right corner shows the output of the denoising process: a large, smooth, low-noise image, which is the final denoised result. It is framed with a green border and contains a small black square at its center, corresponding to the highlighted region in the input image. This image is produced by averaging predictions across neighboring patches, as implied by the caption. The entire figure uses consistent visual elements: grayscale boxes for modules, colored pixel grids for images, arrows for data flow, and clear labels for each component. The layout follows a top-down, left-to-right workflow, emphasizing the transition from noisy input to clean output through neural compression."
}

